INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$52b31d7b$SATQ$, $SATQ$6cd41d96-3454-462f-8b1b-c2293ecdbde2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ab40ba12-49bb-4ce3-82a6-8e2990b2d235$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In November 1934, Amrita Sher-Gil was living in what must have seemed like the ideal city for a young artist: Paris. She was studying firsthand the color-saturated style of France&rsquo;s modernist masters and beginning to make a name for herself as a painter. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Sher-Gil longed to return to her childhood home of India; only there, she believed, could her art truly flourish.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"79085794-445d-4ceb-b05d-0896e870de7c","content":"<p>Still,</p>"},{"id":"a259d760-c73f-49b0-b830-4a3ca72c0c7d","content":"<p>Therefore,</p>"},{"id":"b7f12ebd-6c19-4334-be3b-30efec2ce4e1","content":"<p>Indeed,</p>"},{"id":"b364b112-f77e-40e0-941c-7a714c2220e1","content":"<p>Furthermore,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["79085794-445d-4ceb-b05d-0896e870de7c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Still&rdquo; logically signals that the information about Sher-Gil in this sentence&mdash;that she longed to leave Paris and return to India&mdash;contrasts with what one would expect after reading about Sher-Gil&rsquo;s experiences in Paris in the previous sentences.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;therefore&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Sher-Gil in this sentence is a result or consequence of the descriptions in the previous sentences. Instead, this information contrasts with what one would expect after reading about Sher-Gil&rsquo;s experiences in Paris. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;indeed&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Sher-Gil in this sentence offers additional emphasis in support of the descriptions in the previous sentences. Instead, this information contrasts with what one would expect after reading about Sher-Gil&rsquo;s experiences in Paris. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;furthermore&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Sher-Gil in this sentence offers additional support for or confirmation of the descriptions in the previous sentences. Instead, this information contrasts with what one would expect after reading about Sher-Gil&rsquo;s experiences in Paris. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-212-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b893ac0a-2a65-431d-96a4-3f55f1f7fd69$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"52b31d7b","external_id":"6cd41d96-3454-462f-8b1b-c2293ecdbde2","vaultid":"ab40ba12-49bb-4ce3-82a6-8e2990b2d235","uId":"d2fb1345-f38e-4a03-8b02-4c9cb624c9c7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In November 1934, Amrita Sher-Gil was living in what must have seemed like the ideal city for a young artist: Paris. She was studying firsthand the color-saturated style of France&rsquo;s modernist masters and beginning to make a name for herself as a painter. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Sher-Gil longed to return to her childhood home of India; only there, she believed, could her art truly flourish.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"79085794-445d-4ceb-b05d-0896e870de7c","content":"<p>Still,</p>"},{"id":"a259d760-c73f-49b0-b830-4a3ca72c0c7d","content":"<p>Therefore,</p>"},{"id":"b7f12ebd-6c19-4334-be3b-30efec2ce4e1","content":"<p>Indeed,</p>"},{"id":"b364b112-f77e-40e0-941c-7a714c2220e1","content":"<p>Furthermore,</p>"}],"keys":["79085794-445d-4ceb-b05d-0896e870de7c"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Still&rdquo; logically signals that the information about Sher-Gil in this sentence&mdash;that she longed to leave Paris and return to India&mdash;contrasts with what one would expect after reading about Sher-Gil&rsquo;s experiences in Paris in the previous sentences.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;therefore&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Sher-Gil in this sentence is a result or consequence of the descriptions in the previous sentences. Instead, this information contrasts with what one would expect after reading about Sher-Gil&rsquo;s experiences in Paris. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;indeed&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Sher-Gil in this sentence offers additional emphasis in support of the descriptions in the previous sentences. Instead, this information contrasts with what one would expect after reading about Sher-Gil&rsquo;s experiences in Paris. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;furthermore&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Sher-Gil in this sentence offers additional support for or confirmation of the descriptions in the previous sentences. Instead, this information contrasts with what one would expect after reading about Sher-Gil&rsquo;s experiences in Paris. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-212-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b893ac0a-2a65-431d-96a4-3f55f1f7fd69","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959327,"updateDate":1691007959327}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959327, 1691007959327),
    ($SATQ$54227b8e$SATQ$, $SATQ$f7d28ed2-d97b-4a68-b9f0-133d2e85d8f8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>The mountain pygmy possum is a mammal species.</li>
<li>Up until 1966, it was believed to be extinct.</li>
<li>That year, a live mountain pygmy possum was identified in the wild in Australia.</li>
<li>The mountain pygmy possum is considered a Lazarus species.</li>
<li>&ldquo;Lazarus species&rdquo; is a term for living species of organisms that were once believed to be extinct.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to define the term &ldquo;Lazarus species&rdquo; and provide an example of one. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish these goals?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0f7cb315-b401-4330-bf76-3aedc380e39b","content":"<p>The term &ldquo;Lazarus species&rdquo; describes a living species of organism, such as the mountain pygmy possum, that was once believed to be extinct.</p>"},{"id":"09d67212-1e20-4524-8f34-36ae9edba48c","content":"<p>One example of a Lazarus species is the mountain pygmy possum, a mammal species that was identified in the wild in Australia in 1966.</p>"},{"id":"70e28051-8eec-4e90-bf83-d23f5cf5b095","content":"<p>The mountain pygmy possum, a species of mammal, was identified in the wild in 1966.</p>"},{"id":"5f19b435-694d-4e54-bfe5-901bed590fb7","content":"<p>Sometimes, a species once believed to be extinct is later found living in the wild.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0f7cb315-b401-4330-bf76-3aedc380e39b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence defines the term "Lazarus species" and provides an example of one, explaining that a Lazarus species is a living organism that was once believed to be extinct and providing the mountain pygmy possum as an example.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence provides an example of a Lazarus species, it doesn&rsquo;t define the term "Lazarus species." Choice C is incorrect. The sentence provides information about the mountain pygmy possum; it doesn&rsquo;t define the term "Lazarus species" or explicitly identify the mountain pygmy possum as a Lazarus species. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence describes the concept of a Lazarus species, it doesn&rsquo;t define the term or provide an example of a Lazarus species.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"54227b8e","external_id":"f7d28ed2-d97b-4a68-b9f0-133d2e85d8f8","vaultid":null,"uId":"10747e29-30ee-43f5-a9cf-2a2e56411424","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The mountain pygmy possum is a mammal species.</li>\n<li>Up until 1966, it was believed to be extinct.</li>\n<li>That year, a live mountain pygmy possum was identified in the wild in Australia.</li>\n<li>The mountain pygmy possum is considered a Lazarus species.</li>\n<li>&ldquo;Lazarus species&rdquo; is a term for living species of organisms that were once believed to be extinct.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to define the term &ldquo;Lazarus species&rdquo; and provide an example of one. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish these goals?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0f7cb315-b401-4330-bf76-3aedc380e39b","content":"<p>The term &ldquo;Lazarus species&rdquo; describes a living species of organism, such as the mountain pygmy possum, that was once believed to be extinct.</p>"},{"id":"09d67212-1e20-4524-8f34-36ae9edba48c","content":"<p>One example of a Lazarus species is the mountain pygmy possum, a mammal species that was identified in the wild in Australia in 1966.</p>"},{"id":"70e28051-8eec-4e90-bf83-d23f5cf5b095","content":"<p>The mountain pygmy possum, a species of mammal, was identified in the wild in 1966.</p>"},{"id":"5f19b435-694d-4e54-bfe5-901bed590fb7","content":"<p>Sometimes, a species once believed to be extinct is later found living in the wild.</p>"}],"keys":["0f7cb315-b401-4330-bf76-3aedc380e39b"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence defines the term \"Lazarus species\" and provides an example of one, explaining that a Lazarus species is a living organism that was once believed to be extinct and providing the mountain pygmy possum as an example.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence provides an example of a Lazarus species, it doesn&rsquo;t define the term \"Lazarus species.\" Choice C is incorrect. The sentence provides information about the mountain pygmy possum; it doesn&rsquo;t define the term \"Lazarus species\" or explicitly identify the mountain pygmy possum as a Lazarus species. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence describes the concept of a Lazarus species, it doesn&rsquo;t define the term or provide an example of a Lazarus species.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061193,"updateDate":1755117061193}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061193, 1755117061193),
    ($SATQ$54c1b2dd$SATQ$, $SATQ$6af26e26-7460-40fe-8610-a136bf84e933$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$48be3c7e-b828-4711-8e87-a236f4b1fc88$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>In 1851, German American artist Emanuel Leutze painted <em>Washington Crossing the Delaware</em>.</li>
<li>His huge painting (149 &times; 255 inches) depicts the first US president crossing a river with soldiers in the Revolutionary War.</li>
<li>In 2019, Cree artist Kent Monkman painted <em>mistik&ocirc;siwak (Wooden Boat People): Resurgence of the People</em>.</li>
<li>Monkman&rsquo;s huge painting (132 &times; 264 inches) was inspired by Leutze&rsquo;s.</li>
<li>It portrays Indigenous people in a boat rescuing refugees.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two paintings. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"27457f46-911c-4f5d-80f8-05a133d68f05","content":"<p>Monkman, a Cree artist, finished his painting in 2019; Leutze, a German American artist, completed his in 1851.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2aa3936b-1c82-4701-8296-50e6c570dae9","content":"<p>Although Monkman&rsquo;s painting was inspired by Leutze&rsquo;s, the people and actions the two paintings portray are very different.</p>"},{"id":"b8203149-b853-490d-97c8-6f9e84564f7d","content":"<p>Leutze&rsquo;s and Monkman&rsquo;s paintings are both huge, measuring 149 &times; 255 inches and 132 &times; 264 inches, respectively.</p>"},{"id":"fe247d99-1d10-447a-b55e-a839ebc427b5","content":"<p>Leutze&rsquo;s painting depicts Revolutionary War soldiers, while Monkman&rsquo;s depicts Indigenous people and refugees.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b8203149-b853-490d-97c8-6f9e84564f7d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between the two paintings, noting that Leutze&rsquo;s painting (which measures 149 &times; 255 inches) and Monkman&rsquo;s painting (which measures 132 &times; 264 inches) are both very large. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence mentions that Monkman&rsquo;s painting was completed in 2019 and Leutze&rsquo;s was completed in 1851; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two paintings.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence acknowledges that one painting was inspired by the other, it emphasizes differences between the two paintings; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between them. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence mentions a difference between the two paintings; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between them. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-790-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$0da6ab39-862b-4638-8573-08cdcfbf240c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"54c1b2dd","external_id":"6af26e26-7460-40fe-8610-a136bf84e933","vaultid":"48be3c7e-b828-4711-8e87-a236f4b1fc88","uId":"6faf61a8-ebb5-40e4-a9b4-d1bece2cc914","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In 1851, German American artist Emanuel Leutze painted <em>Washington Crossing the Delaware</em>.</li>\n<li>His huge painting (149 &times; 255 inches) depicts the first US president crossing a river with soldiers in the Revolutionary War.</li>\n<li>In 2019, Cree artist Kent Monkman painted <em>mistik&ocirc;siwak (Wooden Boat People): Resurgence of the People</em>.</li>\n<li>Monkman&rsquo;s huge painting (132 &times; 264 inches) was inspired by Leutze&rsquo;s.</li>\n<li>It portrays Indigenous people in a boat rescuing refugees.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two paintings. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"27457f46-911c-4f5d-80f8-05a133d68f05","content":"<p>Monkman, a Cree artist, finished his painting in 2019; Leutze, a German American artist, completed his in 1851.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2aa3936b-1c82-4701-8296-50e6c570dae9","content":"<p>Although Monkman&rsquo;s painting was inspired by Leutze&rsquo;s, the people and actions the two paintings portray are very different.</p>"},{"id":"b8203149-b853-490d-97c8-6f9e84564f7d","content":"<p>Leutze&rsquo;s and Monkman&rsquo;s paintings are both huge, measuring 149 &times; 255 inches and 132 &times; 264 inches, respectively.</p>"},{"id":"fe247d99-1d10-447a-b55e-a839ebc427b5","content":"<p>Leutze&rsquo;s painting depicts Revolutionary War soldiers, while Monkman&rsquo;s depicts Indigenous people and refugees.</p>"}],"keys":["b8203149-b853-490d-97c8-6f9e84564f7d"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between the two paintings, noting that Leutze&rsquo;s painting (which measures 149 &times; 255 inches) and Monkman&rsquo;s painting (which measures 132 &times; 264 inches) are both very large. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence mentions that Monkman&rsquo;s painting was completed in 2019 and Leutze&rsquo;s was completed in 1851; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two paintings.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence acknowledges that one painting was inspired by the other, it emphasizes differences between the two paintings; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between them. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence mentions a difference between the two paintings; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between them. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-790-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"0da6ab39-862b-4638-8573-08cdcfbf240c","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959353,"updateDate":1691007959353}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959353, 1691007959353),
    ($SATQ$54f29331$SATQ$, $SATQ$72987eb3-56d3-46d4-a4f3-2e43b7affd79$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>The green iguana is a species of reptile.</li>
<li>It can be found in Central America and Brazil.</li>
<li>The green iguana primarily eats leaves and fruit.</li>
<li>It has an average length of 4.8 feet.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to specify the average length of the green iguana. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"01c7e764-e0b8-428f-a1da-3cb484a7e230","content":"<p>The green iguana can be found in Central America.</p>"},{"id":"83059aab-0aa0-4c8c-8a90-9d387dbf49dd","content":"<p>The green iguana has an average length of 4.8 feet.</p>"},{"id":"6c3dc07a-40b8-4a0b-84b1-c98ddd140616","content":"<p>One species of reptile found in Brazil primarily eats leaves and fruit.</p>"},{"id":"f8a8fe49-975c-4eaa-a4b9-02f0e98dea9b","content":"<p>The green iguana is a reptile that primarily eats leaves and fruit.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["83059aab-0aa0-4c8c-8a90-9d387dbf49dd"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence specifies the average length of the green iguana: 4.8 feet.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about the green iguana, it doesn&rsquo;t specify the green iguana&rsquo;s average length. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence describes the diet of a species of reptile in Brazil; it doesn&rsquo;t specify the green iguana&rsquo;s average length. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about the green iguana, it doesn&rsquo;t specify the green iguana&rsquo;s average length.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"54f29331","external_id":"72987eb3-56d3-46d4-a4f3-2e43b7affd79","vaultid":null,"uId":"bf09484c-100f-45b2-b286-c948ac5910e4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The green iguana is a species of reptile.</li>\n<li>It can be found in Central America and Brazil.</li>\n<li>The green iguana primarily eats leaves and fruit.</li>\n<li>It has an average length of 4.8 feet.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to specify the average length of the green iguana. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"01c7e764-e0b8-428f-a1da-3cb484a7e230","content":"<p>The green iguana can be found in Central America.</p>"},{"id":"83059aab-0aa0-4c8c-8a90-9d387dbf49dd","content":"<p>The green iguana has an average length of 4.8 feet.</p>"},{"id":"6c3dc07a-40b8-4a0b-84b1-c98ddd140616","content":"<p>One species of reptile found in Brazil primarily eats leaves and fruit.</p>"},{"id":"f8a8fe49-975c-4eaa-a4b9-02f0e98dea9b","content":"<p>The green iguana is a reptile that primarily eats leaves and fruit.</p>"}],"keys":["83059aab-0aa0-4c8c-8a90-9d387dbf49dd"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence specifies the average length of the green iguana: 4.8 feet.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about the green iguana, it doesn&rsquo;t specify the green iguana&rsquo;s average length. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence describes the diet of a species of reptile in Brazil; it doesn&rsquo;t specify the green iguana&rsquo;s average length. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about the green iguana, it doesn&rsquo;t specify the green iguana&rsquo;s average length.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669208,"updateDate":1743430669208}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669208, 1743430669208),
    ($SATQ$56336696$SATQ$, $SATQ$1af2e817-7908-41f5-bed6-74c57cdac15d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>When using sumac for dyeing cloth, textile artists first soak it in water to release its color. Then, they add the cloth to the dyebath and simmer it for hours, perhaps even days. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> they will remove the cloth, at which point it will have turned a vibrant red color.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e1510995-3973-4eae-8c85-ef6b61b7152c","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"d9113aeb-d770-43ed-bd0f-6a3aad97aad0","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"},{"id":"ad4a8354-d73d-4996-990a-363a035c367a","content":"<p>Eventually,</p>"},{"id":"35894e78-9103-4f09-b1bb-2da9edcade18","content":"<p>In other words,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ad4a8354-d73d-4996-990a-363a035c367a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. "Eventually" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that textile artists will remove the cloth when it has turned red&mdash;is the final step in the chronological sequence of the dyeing process described in the previous sentences.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "nevertheless" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is true despite the previous information about simmering the cloth in the dyebath. Instead, the removal of the cloth is the final step in the dyeing process. Choice B is incorrect because "likewise" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is similar to the previous information about simmering the cloth in the dyebath. Instead, the removal of the cloth is the final step in the dyeing process. Choice D is incorrect because "in other words" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a paraphrase or restatement of the previous information about simmering the cloth in the dyebath. Instead, the removal of the cloth is the final step in the dyeing process.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"56336696","external_id":"1af2e817-7908-41f5-bed6-74c57cdac15d","vaultid":null,"uId":"b62bbe9c-87d5-489d-be8d-0163daa03621","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>When using sumac for dyeing cloth, textile artists first soak it in water to release its color. Then, they add the cloth to the dyebath and simmer it for hours, perhaps even days. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> they will remove the cloth, at which point it will have turned a vibrant red color.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e1510995-3973-4eae-8c85-ef6b61b7152c","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"d9113aeb-d770-43ed-bd0f-6a3aad97aad0","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"},{"id":"ad4a8354-d73d-4996-990a-363a035c367a","content":"<p>Eventually,</p>"},{"id":"35894e78-9103-4f09-b1bb-2da9edcade18","content":"<p>In other words,</p>"}],"keys":["ad4a8354-d73d-4996-990a-363a035c367a"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. \"Eventually\" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that textile artists will remove the cloth when it has turned red&mdash;is the final step in the chronological sequence of the dyeing process described in the previous sentences.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"nevertheless\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is true despite the previous information about simmering the cloth in the dyebath. Instead, the removal of the cloth is the final step in the dyeing process. Choice B is incorrect because \"likewise\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is similar to the previous information about simmering the cloth in the dyebath. Instead, the removal of the cloth is the final step in the dyeing process. Choice D is incorrect because \"in other words\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a paraphrase or restatement of the previous information about simmering the cloth in the dyebath. Instead, the removal of the cloth is the final step in the dyeing process.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061175,"updateDate":1755117061175}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061175, 1755117061175),
    ($SATQ$5645f119$SATQ$, $SATQ$d49db9eb-fd01-4f5e-8520-79aad46ad0c7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>In a 2023 study, environmental scientist Jazmin Locke-Rodriguez and colleagues tested the use of floating treatment wetlands (FTWs) in Florida.</li>
<li>FTWs are artificial floating platforms of plants used to remediate polluted or nutrient-imbalanced water.</li>
<li>Finding: FTWs using marigold flowers removed 52% more total phosphorus than the control.</li>
<li>Finding: The test yielded 65 market-quality blooms per square meter.</li>
<li>The authors concluded marigolds showed &ldquo;promising potential as a commercially viable remediating crop cultivated on FTWs in South Florida.&rdquo;</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to present the findings of the study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ce876b41-2681-4114-ab00-c16946c369e4","content":"<p>The authors concluded that marigolds grown on FTWs were &ldquo;commercially viable,&rdquo; having produced 65 blooms per square meter of market-quality blooms in a 2023 study.</p>"},{"id":"42e2b0bf-7907-436a-87ea-ee7b6b8ecb82","content":"<p>In a 2023 study, Locke-Rodriguez and colleagues found that marigolds cultivated on FTWs produced 52% more market-quality flower blooms than the control.</p>"},{"id":"179887fb-cb7d-408d-8870-4ac5250df844","content":"<p>Locke-Rodriguez and colleagues found that FTWs using marigolds not only helped remove phosphorus from the water but also yielded market-quality blooms.</p>"},{"id":"ec91d1d3-fc0b-4349-9b61-e3df149249b0","content":"<p>FTWs using marigolds, Locke-Rodriguez and colleagues found, yielded 65 flower blooms and removed 52% of phosphorus from the water.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["179887fb-cb7d-408d-8870-4ac5250df844"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence presents both findings from the study, noting that FTWs using marigolds both removed phosphorus from the water and yielded market-quality blooms.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence presents a finding from the study, the presentation is incomplete; the study found that marigolds cultivated on FTWs both removed phosphorus from the water and yielded market-quality blooms. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents information in the notes. The figure of 52% refers to the amount of phosphorus removed by FTWs using marigolds when compared to the control; the marigolds&rsquo; bloom yield was 65 market-quality blooms per square meter, and it was not compared to the control. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents information in the notes. The FTWs using marigolds removed 52% more phosphorus from the water than did the control, not 52% of the total phosphorus. Additionally, the bloom figure is incomplete; the figure is 65 market-quality flower blooms per square meter.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5645f119","external_id":"d49db9eb-fd01-4f5e-8520-79aad46ad0c7","vaultid":null,"uId":"a3f24c8b-6d21-44a9-9539-a39a612e4060","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In a 2023 study, environmental scientist Jazmin Locke-Rodriguez and colleagues tested the use of floating treatment wetlands (FTWs) in Florida.</li>\n<li>FTWs are artificial floating platforms of plants used to remediate polluted or nutrient-imbalanced water.</li>\n<li>Finding: FTWs using marigold flowers removed 52% more total phosphorus than the control.</li>\n<li>Finding: The test yielded 65 market-quality blooms per square meter.</li>\n<li>The authors concluded marigolds showed &ldquo;promising potential as a commercially viable remediating crop cultivated on FTWs in South Florida.&rdquo;</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to present the findings of the study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ce876b41-2681-4114-ab00-c16946c369e4","content":"<p>The authors concluded that marigolds grown on FTWs were &ldquo;commercially viable,&rdquo; having produced 65 blooms per square meter of market-quality blooms in a 2023 study.</p>"},{"id":"42e2b0bf-7907-436a-87ea-ee7b6b8ecb82","content":"<p>In a 2023 study, Locke-Rodriguez and colleagues found that marigolds cultivated on FTWs produced 52% more market-quality flower blooms than the control.</p>"},{"id":"179887fb-cb7d-408d-8870-4ac5250df844","content":"<p>Locke-Rodriguez and colleagues found that FTWs using marigolds not only helped remove phosphorus from the water but also yielded market-quality blooms.</p>"},{"id":"ec91d1d3-fc0b-4349-9b61-e3df149249b0","content":"<p>FTWs using marigolds, Locke-Rodriguez and colleagues found, yielded 65 flower blooms and removed 52% of phosphorus from the water.</p>"}],"keys":["179887fb-cb7d-408d-8870-4ac5250df844"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence presents both findings from the study, noting that FTWs using marigolds both removed phosphorus from the water and yielded market-quality blooms.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence presents a finding from the study, the presentation is incomplete; the study found that marigolds cultivated on FTWs both removed phosphorus from the water and yielded market-quality blooms. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents information in the notes. The figure of 52% refers to the amount of phosphorus removed by FTWs using marigolds when compared to the control; the marigolds&rsquo; bloom yield was 65 market-quality blooms per square meter, and it was not compared to the control. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents information in the notes. The FTWs using marigolds removed 52% more phosphorus from the water than did the control, not 52% of the total phosphorus. Additionally, the bloom figure is incomplete; the figure is 65 market-quality flower blooms per square meter.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061192,"updateDate":1755117061192}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061192, 1755117061192),
    ($SATQ$56b000d0$SATQ$, $SATQ$514e4a8c-074b-4212-9e5b-95cd02f9d16d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$2b7b3d91-52cd-4f8a-a068-adb1bf600326$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>The factors that affect clutch size (the number of eggs laid at one time) have been well studied in birds but not in lizards.</li>
<li>A team led by Shai Meiri of Tel Aviv University investigated which factors influence lizard clutch size.</li>
<li>Meiri&rsquo;s team obtained clutch-size and habitat data for over 3,900 lizard species and analyzed the data with statistical models.</li>
<li>Larger clutch size was associated with environments in higher latitudes that have more seasonal change.</li>
<li>Lizards in higher-latitude environments may lay larger clutches to take advantage of shorter windows of favorable conditions.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize the aim of the research study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9ccbcde1-ce43-4735-9c23-71b3328f3b45","content":"<p>Researchers wanted to know which factors influence lizard egg clutch size because such factors have been well studied in birds but not in lizards.</p>"},{"id":"a8e07c5a-f430-4e9b-a7a8-570d8eeb9661","content":"<p>After they obtained data for over 3,900 lizard species, researchers determined that larger clutch size was associated with environments in higher latitudes that have more seasonal change.</p>"},{"id":"158e89a3-e145-4bf7-a7d4-aa0b8d69f4ad","content":"<p>We now know that lizards in higher-latitude environments may lay larger clutches to take advantage of shorter windows of favorable conditions.</p>"},{"id":"40615ea7-526a-4cbb-9127-9b78472f7e5f","content":"<p>Researchers obtained clutch-size and habitat data for over 3,900 lizard species and analyzed the data with statistical models.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9ccbcde1-ce43-4735-9c23-71b3328f3b45"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the aim of the research study by highlighting what the researchers conducting the study wanted to know&mdash;specifically, which factors influence clutch size among lizards. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the sentence emphasizes what researchers determined at the end of the study, not what the study&rsquo;s aim was. Choice C is incorrect because the sentence emphasizes a finding from the research study, not the aim of the study. Choice D is incorrect because the sentence emphasizes the research study&rsquo;s methodology, not its aim. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-725-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$31f205ae-d815-4c4f-ab2d-57e7693c300b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"56b000d0","external_id":"514e4a8c-074b-4212-9e5b-95cd02f9d16d","vaultid":"2b7b3d91-52cd-4f8a-a068-adb1bf600326","uId":"fbbb13a3-8406-400c-8f3d-fc4f7f97e358","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The factors that affect clutch size (the number of eggs laid at one time) have been well studied in birds but not in lizards.</li>\n<li>A team led by Shai Meiri of Tel Aviv University investigated which factors influence lizard clutch size.</li>\n<li>Meiri&rsquo;s team obtained clutch-size and habitat data for over 3,900 lizard species and analyzed the data with statistical models.</li>\n<li>Larger clutch size was associated with environments in higher latitudes that have more seasonal change.</li>\n<li>Lizards in higher-latitude environments may lay larger clutches to take advantage of shorter windows of favorable conditions.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize the aim of the research study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9ccbcde1-ce43-4735-9c23-71b3328f3b45","content":"<p>Researchers wanted to know which factors influence lizard egg clutch size because such factors have been well studied in birds but not in lizards.</p>"},{"id":"a8e07c5a-f430-4e9b-a7a8-570d8eeb9661","content":"<p>After they obtained data for over 3,900 lizard species, researchers determined that larger clutch size was associated with environments in higher latitudes that have more seasonal change.</p>"},{"id":"158e89a3-e145-4bf7-a7d4-aa0b8d69f4ad","content":"<p>We now know that lizards in higher-latitude environments may lay larger clutches to take advantage of shorter windows of favorable conditions.</p>"},{"id":"40615ea7-526a-4cbb-9127-9b78472f7e5f","content":"<p>Researchers obtained clutch-size and habitat data for over 3,900 lizard species and analyzed the data with statistical models.</p>"}],"keys":["9ccbcde1-ce43-4735-9c23-71b3328f3b45"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the aim of the research study by highlighting what the researchers conducting the study wanted to know&mdash;specifically, which factors influence clutch size among lizards. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the sentence emphasizes what researchers determined at the end of the study, not what the study&rsquo;s aim was. Choice C is incorrect because the sentence emphasizes a finding from the research study, not the aim of the study. Choice D is incorrect because the sentence emphasizes the research study&rsquo;s methodology, not its aim. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-725-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"31f205ae-d815-4c4f-ab2d-57e7693c300b","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959349,"updateDate":1691007959349}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959349, 1691007959349),
    ($SATQ$56cad44a$SATQ$, $SATQ$e29c0a06-1d44-4568-9459-7f708ecebbca$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$314e25d4-06ab-44a9-9425-52131a693e21$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Mexican tetras are a fish species with two distinct populations.</li>
<li>Surface-dwelling tetras live on the surface and are able to see.</li>
<li>Cave-dwelling tetras live in total darkness and have lost the ability to see.</li>
<li>Cave-dwelling tetras have asymmetrical skulls with more sensory receptors on one side than the other.</li>
<li>These receptors help cave-dwelling tetras navigate in darkness.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize a difference between surface-dwelling and cave-dwelling tetras. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"21e31d95-130a-4d24-ac45-f9b81ceb2b89","content":"<p>Surface-dwelling and cave-dwelling tetras may belong to the same species, but they are quite different.</p>"},{"id":"7193d850-3d95-4ede-a06e-eadf8b682971","content":"<p>Cave-dwelling tetras can no longer see but use sensory receptors on their skulls to navigate.</p>"},{"id":"c95d2b24-ca5e-4571-b5aa-2ab146e072de","content":"<p>Mexican tetras are a fish species with two distinct populations: surface-dwelling tetras and cave-dwelling tetras.</p>"},{"id":"41da5280-2f7e-4e66-b2fa-b4e2bccc54c4","content":"<p>Surface-dwelling tetras can see, whereas cave-dwelling tetras cannot.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["41da5280-2f7e-4e66-b2fa-b4e2bccc54c4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes a difference between surface-dwelling and cave-dwelling tetras, noting that while surface-dwelling tetras can see, cave-dwelling tetras can&rsquo;t. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence notes that surface-dwelling and cave-dwelling tetras are different, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize any difference between the two populations of tetras. Choice B is incorrect because the sentence explains that cave-dwelling tetras use sensory receptors on their skulls to navigate; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a difference between surface-dwelling and cave-dwelling tetras. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence notes that there are two different populations of Mexican tetras, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize any difference between the two populations. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-726-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$92274c90-1231-4e7f-9cf9-1a7c83006242$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"56cad44a","external_id":"e29c0a06-1d44-4568-9459-7f708ecebbca","vaultid":"314e25d4-06ab-44a9-9425-52131a693e21","uId":"79417f38-3f6d-40cb-b7a1-cdc3f51e8bd7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mexican tetras are a fish species with two distinct populations.</li>\n<li>Surface-dwelling tetras live on the surface and are able to see.</li>\n<li>Cave-dwelling tetras live in total darkness and have lost the ability to see.</li>\n<li>Cave-dwelling tetras have asymmetrical skulls with more sensory receptors on one side than the other.</li>\n<li>These receptors help cave-dwelling tetras navigate in darkness.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize a difference between surface-dwelling and cave-dwelling tetras. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"21e31d95-130a-4d24-ac45-f9b81ceb2b89","content":"<p>Surface-dwelling and cave-dwelling tetras may belong to the same species, but they are quite different.</p>"},{"id":"7193d850-3d95-4ede-a06e-eadf8b682971","content":"<p>Cave-dwelling tetras can no longer see but use sensory receptors on their skulls to navigate.</p>"},{"id":"c95d2b24-ca5e-4571-b5aa-2ab146e072de","content":"<p>Mexican tetras are a fish species with two distinct populations: surface-dwelling tetras and cave-dwelling tetras.</p>"},{"id":"41da5280-2f7e-4e66-b2fa-b4e2bccc54c4","content":"<p>Surface-dwelling tetras can see, whereas cave-dwelling tetras cannot.</p>"}],"keys":["41da5280-2f7e-4e66-b2fa-b4e2bccc54c4"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes a difference between surface-dwelling and cave-dwelling tetras, noting that while surface-dwelling tetras can see, cave-dwelling tetras can&rsquo;t. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence notes that surface-dwelling and cave-dwelling tetras are different, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize any difference between the two populations of tetras. Choice B is incorrect because the sentence explains that cave-dwelling tetras use sensory receptors on their skulls to navigate; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a difference between surface-dwelling and cave-dwelling tetras. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence notes that there are two different populations of Mexican tetras, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize any difference between the two populations. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-726-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"92274c90-1231-4e7f-9cf9-1a7c83006242","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959349,"updateDate":1691007959349}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959349, 1691007959349),
    ($SATQ$570dd854$SATQ$, $SATQ$a9b0711d-497a-4790-924b-3ba7c43e2b09$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>Here I Have Returned</em> is a sculpture by Egyptian American artist Sherin Guirguis.</li>
<li>It is a large, curved strip of wood inspired by the shape of a sistrum.</li>
<li>A sistrum is a curved musical instrument played by ancient Egyptian priestesses in ceremonies.</li>
<li>Guirguis says that the sculpture symbolizes &ldquo;women who have lifted and supported Egyptian society and culture.&rdquo;</li>
<li>Overall, Guirguis wants her works to &ldquo;engage audiences in a dialogue about power, agency, and social transformation.&rdquo;</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to use a quotation from Guirguis to explain what the sculpture represents. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6d226a3c-7e58-44c2-a76c-62dff4596cbd","content":"<p>Guirguis, whose works include a sculpture that is a large, curved strip of wood, has explained that she wants her work to create a dialogue with audiences.</p>"},{"id":"4d9b2fca-af50-4fc5-9a08-47c27d6e2de8","content":"<p>Inspired by the sistrum played by Egyptian priestesses, <em>Here I Have Returned</em> symbolizes &ldquo;women who have lifted and supported Egyptian society and culture,&rdquo; according to Guirguis.</p>"},{"id":"a662eb2c-b98b-41a8-93bd-4e4275862523","content":"<p>According to Guirguis, the curved strip of wood used in <em>Here I Have Returned</em> was inspired by the sistrum, a musical instrument played by ancient Egyptian priestesses in ceremonies.</p>"},{"id":"990d8630-6e9e-4dd3-8140-86cc3752c3ab","content":"<p>Guirguis, the sculptor of <em>Here I Have Returned</em>, wants her works to &ldquo;engage audiences in a dialogue about power, agency, and social transformation.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4d9b2fca-af50-4fc5-9a08-47c27d6e2de8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence uses a quotation from Guirguis to explain what <em>Here I Have Returned</em> represents, noting that Guirguis said the sculpture symbolizes "women who have lifted and supported Egyptian society and culture."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence explains what Guirguis says she wants her work to achieve and provides an example of her work; it doesn&rsquo;t use a quotation to explain what the sculpture represents. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence mentions the instrument whose shape inspired the sculpture but doesn&rsquo;t use a quotation to explain what the sculpture represents. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence does use a quotation from Guirguis, the quotation explains what she hopes her works in general achieve, not what the sculpture in particular represents.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"570dd854","external_id":"a9b0711d-497a-4790-924b-3ba7c43e2b09","vaultid":null,"uId":"f37be0a7-bef3-45fc-9a6a-132c4982490a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Here I Have Returned</em> is a sculpture by Egyptian American artist Sherin Guirguis.</li>\n<li>It is a large, curved strip of wood inspired by the shape of a sistrum.</li>\n<li>A sistrum is a curved musical instrument played by ancient Egyptian priestesses in ceremonies.</li>\n<li>Guirguis says that the sculpture symbolizes &ldquo;women who have lifted and supported Egyptian society and culture.&rdquo;</li>\n<li>Overall, Guirguis wants her works to &ldquo;engage audiences in a dialogue about power, agency, and social transformation.&rdquo;</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to use a quotation from Guirguis to explain what the sculpture represents. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6d226a3c-7e58-44c2-a76c-62dff4596cbd","content":"<p>Guirguis, whose works include a sculpture that is a large, curved strip of wood, has explained that she wants her work to create a dialogue with audiences.</p>"},{"id":"4d9b2fca-af50-4fc5-9a08-47c27d6e2de8","content":"<p>Inspired by the sistrum played by Egyptian priestesses, <em>Here I Have Returned</em> symbolizes &ldquo;women who have lifted and supported Egyptian society and culture,&rdquo; according to Guirguis.</p>"},{"id":"a662eb2c-b98b-41a8-93bd-4e4275862523","content":"<p>According to Guirguis, the curved strip of wood used in <em>Here I Have Returned</em> was inspired by the sistrum, a musical instrument played by ancient Egyptian priestesses in ceremonies.</p>"},{"id":"990d8630-6e9e-4dd3-8140-86cc3752c3ab","content":"<p>Guirguis, the sculptor of <em>Here I Have Returned</em>, wants her works to &ldquo;engage audiences in a dialogue about power, agency, and social transformation.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["4d9b2fca-af50-4fc5-9a08-47c27d6e2de8"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence uses a quotation from Guirguis to explain what <em>Here I Have Returned</em> represents, noting that Guirguis said the sculpture symbolizes \"women who have lifted and supported Egyptian society and culture.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence explains what Guirguis says she wants her work to achieve and provides an example of her work; it doesn&rsquo;t use a quotation to explain what the sculpture represents. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence mentions the instrument whose shape inspired the sculpture but doesn&rsquo;t use a quotation to explain what the sculpture represents. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence does use a quotation from Guirguis, the quotation explains what she hopes her works in general achieve, not what the sculpture in particular represents.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323034,"updateDate":1730147323034}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323034, 1730147323034),
    ($SATQ$57bcd0d6$SATQ$, $SATQ$db0c09d4-1861-4717-ab9d-e1839b45041b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$5ba1f2a8-9609-42fe-9b05-3d36dfe5b42e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Etched into Peru&rsquo;s Nazca Desert are line drawings so large that they can only be fully seen from high above. Archaeologists have known of the lines since the 1920s, when a researcher spotted some from a nearby foothill, and they have been studying the markings ever since. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> archaeologists&rsquo; efforts are aided by drones that capture high-resolution aerial photographs of the lines.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"dcb001e3-7f7e-4b1e-85a7-3afbd445da1d","content":"<p>Currently,</p>"},{"id":"0afd615c-6eb3-41df-8cc5-b4153864c9d4","content":"<p>In comparison,</p>"},{"id":"15fb8e84-6117-4243-ba99-ce379a4ab0da","content":"<p>Still,</p>"},{"id":"22d3e18a-efcb-4450-98e3-b7bfb65a8fb0","content":"<p>However,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["dcb001e3-7f7e-4b1e-85a7-3afbd445da1d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Currently&rdquo; logically signals that the archaeologists&rsquo; use of drones (a current technology) to photograph the lines is the present-day continuation of the ongoing archaeological research described in the previous sentence. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;in comparison&rdquo; illogically signals that the action described in this sentence offers a comparison to the ongoing archaeological research described in the previous sentence. Instead, the use of drones is the present-day continuation of that research. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;still&rdquo; illogically signals that the action described in this sentence occurs despite the ongoing archaeological research described in the previous sentence. Instead, the use of drones is the present-day continuation of that research. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;however&rdquo; illogically signals that the action described in this sentence occurs either despite or in contrast to the ongoing archaeological research described in the previous sentence. Instead, the use of drones is the present-day continuation of that research. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-219-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$85b60d24-a897-40c0-a79d-622b0f1d5251$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"57bcd0d6","external_id":"db0c09d4-1861-4717-ab9d-e1839b45041b","vaultid":"5ba1f2a8-9609-42fe-9b05-3d36dfe5b42e","uId":"28c3dad9-aa6b-4602-b447-00813a910f6d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Etched into Peru&rsquo;s Nazca Desert are line drawings so large that they can only be fully seen from high above. Archaeologists have known of the lines since the 1920s, when a researcher spotted some from a nearby foothill, and they have been studying the markings ever since. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> archaeologists&rsquo; efforts are aided by drones that capture high-resolution aerial photographs of the lines.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"dcb001e3-7f7e-4b1e-85a7-3afbd445da1d","content":"<p>Currently,</p>"},{"id":"0afd615c-6eb3-41df-8cc5-b4153864c9d4","content":"<p>In comparison,</p>"},{"id":"15fb8e84-6117-4243-ba99-ce379a4ab0da","content":"<p>Still,</p>"},{"id":"22d3e18a-efcb-4450-98e3-b7bfb65a8fb0","content":"<p>However,</p>"}],"keys":["dcb001e3-7f7e-4b1e-85a7-3afbd445da1d"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Currently&rdquo; logically signals that the archaeologists&rsquo; use of drones (a current technology) to photograph the lines is the present-day continuation of the ongoing archaeological research described in the previous sentence. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;in comparison&rdquo; illogically signals that the action described in this sentence offers a comparison to the ongoing archaeological research described in the previous sentence. Instead, the use of drones is the present-day continuation of that research. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;still&rdquo; illogically signals that the action described in this sentence occurs despite the ongoing archaeological research described in the previous sentence. Instead, the use of drones is the present-day continuation of that research. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;however&rdquo; illogically signals that the action described in this sentence occurs either despite or in contrast to the ongoing archaeological research described in the previous sentence. Instead, the use of drones is the present-day continuation of that research. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-219-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"85b60d24-a897-40c0-a79d-622b0f1d5251","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959327,"updateDate":1691007959327}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959327, 1691007959327),
    ($SATQ$58281fc4$SATQ$, $SATQ$7a0e073a-88e5-400e-9036-41e35d7aef5b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$679fdcf1-863c-420d-b403-71e9c341e2d1$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Soo Sunny Park is a Korean American artist who uses light as her primary medium of expression.</li>
<li>She created her work <em>Unwoven Light</em> in 2013.</li>
<li><em>Unwoven Light </em>featured a chain-link fence fitted with iridescent plexiglass tiles.</li>
<li>When light passed through the fence, colorful prisms formed.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to describe <em>Unwoven Light</em> to an audience unfamiliar with Soo Sunny Park. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"30cc6857-98af-4eb3-95e8-bfd4fcf10f77","content":"<p>Park&rsquo;s 2013 installation <em>Unwoven Light</em>, which included a chain-link fence and iridescent tiles made from plexiglass, featured light as its primary medium of expression.</p>"},{"id":"094f27ea-1fd4-4658-b19b-40815b38c2cb","content":"<p>Korean American light artist Soo Sunny Park created <em>Unwoven Light </em>in 2013.</p>"},{"id":"70bdf068-9600-401f-add7-eb6cd97018d6","content":"<p>The chain-link fence in Soo Sunny Park&rsquo;s <em>Unwoven Light</em> was fitted with tiles made from iridescent plexiglass.</p>"},{"id":"a61b988d-1c0d-4306-98ef-849d13a0be8b","content":"<p>In <em>Unwoven Light</em>, a 2013 work by Korean American artist Soo Sunny Park<em>,</em> light formed colorful prisms as it passed through a fence Park had fitted with iridescent tiles.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a61b988d-1c0d-4306-98ef-849d13a0be8b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence effectively describes <em>Unwoven Light</em> to an audience unfamiliar with Park, noting that Soo Sunny Park is a Korean American artist and that the 2013 work consists of colorful prisms formed by light passing through iridescent tiles. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes aspects of <em>Unwoven Light</em> but doesn&rsquo;t mention who Park is; it thus doesn&rsquo;t effectively describe the work to an audience unfamiliar with Park. Choice B is incorrect. Although the sentence indicates when the work was created and who Park is, it lacks descriptive details and thus doesn&rsquo;t effectively describe <em>Unwoven Light</em>.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. The sentence mentions Park and describes an aspect of <em>Unwoven Light</em>&mdash;the chain-link fence&mdash;but doesn&rsquo;t effectively describe the overall work to an audience unfamiliar with the artist. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-743-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$fa06562b-656b-477d-a41a-ef06e1a9b431$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"58281fc4","external_id":"7a0e073a-88e5-400e-9036-41e35d7aef5b","vaultid":"679fdcf1-863c-420d-b403-71e9c341e2d1","uId":"67b731a7-0963-4b26-a0a0-e506d278209c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Soo Sunny Park is a Korean American artist who uses light as her primary medium of expression.</li>\n<li>She created her work <em>Unwoven Light</em> in 2013.</li>\n<li><em>Unwoven Light </em>featured a chain-link fence fitted with iridescent plexiglass tiles.</li>\n<li>When light passed through the fence, colorful prisms formed.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to describe <em>Unwoven Light</em> to an audience unfamiliar with Soo Sunny Park. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"30cc6857-98af-4eb3-95e8-bfd4fcf10f77","content":"<p>Park&rsquo;s 2013 installation <em>Unwoven Light</em>, which included a chain-link fence and iridescent tiles made from plexiglass, featured light as its primary medium of expression.</p>"},{"id":"094f27ea-1fd4-4658-b19b-40815b38c2cb","content":"<p>Korean American light artist Soo Sunny Park created <em>Unwoven Light </em>in 2013.</p>"},{"id":"70bdf068-9600-401f-add7-eb6cd97018d6","content":"<p>The chain-link fence in Soo Sunny Park&rsquo;s <em>Unwoven Light</em> was fitted with tiles made from iridescent plexiglass.</p>"},{"id":"a61b988d-1c0d-4306-98ef-849d13a0be8b","content":"<p>In <em>Unwoven Light</em>, a 2013 work by Korean American artist Soo Sunny Park<em>,</em> light formed colorful prisms as it passed through a fence Park had fitted with iridescent tiles.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["a61b988d-1c0d-4306-98ef-849d13a0be8b"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence effectively describes <em>Unwoven Light</em> to an audience unfamiliar with Park, noting that Soo Sunny Park is a Korean American artist and that the 2013 work consists of colorful prisms formed by light passing through iridescent tiles. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes aspects of <em>Unwoven Light</em> but doesn&rsquo;t mention who Park is; it thus doesn&rsquo;t effectively describe the work to an audience unfamiliar with Park. Choice B is incorrect. Although the sentence indicates when the work was created and who Park is, it lacks descriptive details and thus doesn&rsquo;t effectively describe <em>Unwoven Light</em>.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. The sentence mentions Park and describes an aspect of <em>Unwoven Light</em>&mdash;the chain-link fence&mdash;but doesn&rsquo;t effectively describe the overall work to an audience unfamiliar with the artist. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-743-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"fa06562b-656b-477d-a41a-ef06e1a9b431","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959350,"updateDate":1691007959350}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959350, 1691007959350),
    ($SATQ$5888712f$SATQ$, $SATQ$5c0a4203-7ee6-4653-abae-ec4b9fca2b90$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$5b463a54-d43b-4557-b4e8-94b1554161d7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Physicist Muluneh Abebe was working on a garment suited for both warm and cold conditions.</li>
<li>He analyzed the emissivity, or ability to emit heat, of the materials he planned to use.</li>
<li>Abebe found that reflective metal fibers emitted almost no heat and had an emissivity of 0.02.</li>
<li>He found that silicon carbide fibers absorbed large amounts of heat and had an emissivity of 0.74.</li>
<li>The amount of heat a material absorbs is equal to the amount of heat it emits.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to contrast the emissivity of reflective metal fibers with that of silicon carbide fibers. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"31fb649e-a765-4d85-b2ec-90d82c1d6ae5","content":"<p>The ability of reflective metal fibers and silicon carbide fibers to emit heat was determined by an analysis of each material&rsquo;s emissivity.</p>"},{"id":"2c377c6a-9e8f-455c-98f6-b8c512a185e6","content":"<p>The amount of heat a material absorbs is equal to the amount it emits, as evidenced in Abebe&rsquo;s analyses.</p>"},{"id":"21055d90-9d3b-4c78-8766-23d3334d747e","content":"<p>Though the reflective metal fibers and silicon carbide fibers had different rates of emissivity, Abebe planned to use both in a garment.</p>"},{"id":"345da25e-8ba2-4af3-9330-227de9a231f5","content":"<p>Whereas the reflective metal fibers had an emissivity of just 0.02, the silicon carbide fibers absorbed large amounts of heat, resulting in an emissivity of 0.74.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["345da25e-8ba2-4af3-9330-227de9a231f5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence uses &ldquo;whereas&rdquo; to contrast the emissivities of the two fibers, noting that the emissivity of the reflective metal fibers was just 0.02, far lower than that of the silicon carbide fibers (0.74). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the ability of reflective metal fibers and silicon carbide fibers to emit heat; it doesn&rsquo;t contrast the emissivities of the two fibers. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence states a law of thermodynamics: the amount of heat a material absorbs is equal to the amount it emits. The sentence doesn&rsquo;t contrast the emissivity of reflective metal fibers with that of silicon carbide fibers. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence includes a generalization about the emissivities of reflective metal fibers and silicon carbide fibers, it emphasizes Abebe&rsquo;s plans for their use in a garment; it doesn&rsquo;t contrast the emissivities of the two fibers. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-776-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$d4cc2fa8-576d-411e-a4d9-2d3ed9d6b1aa$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5888712f","external_id":"5c0a4203-7ee6-4653-abae-ec4b9fca2b90","vaultid":"5b463a54-d43b-4557-b4e8-94b1554161d7","uId":"8270a482-b2b0-4579-b525-cdb10a9b0957","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Physicist Muluneh Abebe was working on a garment suited for both warm and cold conditions.</li>\n<li>He analyzed the emissivity, or ability to emit heat, of the materials he planned to use.</li>\n<li>Abebe found that reflective metal fibers emitted almost no heat and had an emissivity of 0.02.</li>\n<li>He found that silicon carbide fibers absorbed large amounts of heat and had an emissivity of 0.74.</li>\n<li>The amount of heat a material absorbs is equal to the amount of heat it emits.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to contrast the emissivity of reflective metal fibers with that of silicon carbide fibers. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"31fb649e-a765-4d85-b2ec-90d82c1d6ae5","content":"<p>The ability of reflective metal fibers and silicon carbide fibers to emit heat was determined by an analysis of each material&rsquo;s emissivity.</p>"},{"id":"2c377c6a-9e8f-455c-98f6-b8c512a185e6","content":"<p>The amount of heat a material absorbs is equal to the amount it emits, as evidenced in Abebe&rsquo;s analyses.</p>"},{"id":"21055d90-9d3b-4c78-8766-23d3334d747e","content":"<p>Though the reflective metal fibers and silicon carbide fibers had different rates of emissivity, Abebe planned to use both in a garment.</p>"},{"id":"345da25e-8ba2-4af3-9330-227de9a231f5","content":"<p>Whereas the reflective metal fibers had an emissivity of just 0.02, the silicon carbide fibers absorbed large amounts of heat, resulting in an emissivity of 0.74.</p>"}],"keys":["345da25e-8ba2-4af3-9330-227de9a231f5"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence uses &ldquo;whereas&rdquo; to contrast the emissivities of the two fibers, noting that the emissivity of the reflective metal fibers was just 0.02, far lower than that of the silicon carbide fibers (0.74). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the ability of reflective metal fibers and silicon carbide fibers to emit heat; it doesn&rsquo;t contrast the emissivities of the two fibers. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence states a law of thermodynamics: the amount of heat a material absorbs is equal to the amount it emits. The sentence doesn&rsquo;t contrast the emissivity of reflective metal fibers with that of silicon carbide fibers. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence includes a generalization about the emissivities of reflective metal fibers and silicon carbide fibers, it emphasizes Abebe&rsquo;s plans for their use in a garment; it doesn&rsquo;t contrast the emissivities of the two fibers. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-776-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"d4cc2fa8-576d-411e-a4d9-2d3ed9d6b1aa","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959352,"updateDate":1691007959352}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959352, 1691007959352),
    ($SATQ$5a2a4b36$SATQ$, $SATQ$4ec44add-b3c3-4555-88cd-83490e01096e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Pauline Hopkins (1859-1930) was an African American writer.</li>
<li>In her career, she created many genre-defining stories.</li>
<li>Her serialized novel <em>Hagar&rsquo;s Daughter</em>&nbsp;was published from 1901 to 1902 in <em>The Colored American Magazine</em>.</li>
<li>It is considered the first African American mystery novel.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to introduce Pauline Hopkins<em>&nbsp;</em>to an audience unfamiliar with her career. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4da3cd4d-0c81-461d-8c4c-68cee263faf7","content":"<p>Pauline Hopkins is the author of <em>Hagar&rsquo;s Daughter</em>, a serialized novel published from 1901 to 1902.</p>"},{"id":"3a6a7c9d-b06c-4398-9e8c-416cbde4079e","content":"<p>African American writer Pauline Hopkins created many genre-defining stories during her career, including the first African American mystery novel, <em>Hagar&rsquo;s Daughter</em>.</p>"},{"id":"31bd03f3-71e7-42e4-82f1-782b29bd4290","content":"<p>Published in <em>The Colored American Magazine </em>from 1901 to 1902<em>, Hagar&rsquo;s Daughter </em>is a serialized novel by Pauline Hopkins.</p>"},{"id":"b7b28a84-f10e-40d7-a8b0-f8a738de9b02","content":"<p><em>Hagar&rsquo;s Daughter </em>by Pauline Hopkins is considered the first African American mystery novel.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3a6a7c9d-b06c-4398-9e8c-416cbde4079e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence introduces Pauline Hopkins to an audience unfamiliar with her career, identifying her as a genre-defining African American writer and noting a career accomplishment.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes that Pauline Hopkins wrote a serialized novel but doesn&rsquo;t introduce Hopkins to an audience unfamiliar with her career. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence introduces Hopkins&rsquo;s novel; it doesn&rsquo;t introduce Hopkins. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence explains that a novel written by Hopkins is considered the first African American mystery novel; it doesn&rsquo;t introduce Hopkins to an audience unfamiliar with her career.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5a2a4b36","external_id":"4ec44add-b3c3-4555-88cd-83490e01096e","vaultid":null,"uId":"3cffd055-62fd-4c59-8d13-cef18ba22bfb","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pauline Hopkins (1859-1930) was an African American writer.</li>\n<li>In her career, she created many genre-defining stories.</li>\n<li>Her serialized novel <em>Hagar&rsquo;s Daughter</em>&nbsp;was published from 1901 to 1902 in <em>The Colored American Magazine</em>.</li>\n<li>It is considered the first African American mystery novel.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to introduce Pauline Hopkins<em>&nbsp;</em>to an audience unfamiliar with her career. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4da3cd4d-0c81-461d-8c4c-68cee263faf7","content":"<p>Pauline Hopkins is the author of <em>Hagar&rsquo;s Daughter</em>, a serialized novel published from 1901 to 1902.</p>"},{"id":"3a6a7c9d-b06c-4398-9e8c-416cbde4079e","content":"<p>African American writer Pauline Hopkins created many genre-defining stories during her career, including the first African American mystery novel, <em>Hagar&rsquo;s Daughter</em>.</p>"},{"id":"31bd03f3-71e7-42e4-82f1-782b29bd4290","content":"<p>Published in <em>The Colored American Magazine </em>from 1901 to 1902<em>, Hagar&rsquo;s Daughter </em>is a serialized novel by Pauline Hopkins.</p>"},{"id":"b7b28a84-f10e-40d7-a8b0-f8a738de9b02","content":"<p><em>Hagar&rsquo;s Daughter </em>by Pauline Hopkins is considered the first African American mystery novel.</p>"}],"keys":["3a6a7c9d-b06c-4398-9e8c-416cbde4079e"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence introduces Pauline Hopkins to an audience unfamiliar with her career, identifying her as a genre-defining African American writer and noting a career accomplishment.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes that Pauline Hopkins wrote a serialized novel but doesn&rsquo;t introduce Hopkins to an audience unfamiliar with her career. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence introduces Hopkins&rsquo;s novel; it doesn&rsquo;t introduce Hopkins. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence explains that a novel written by Hopkins is considered the first African American mystery novel; it doesn&rsquo;t introduce Hopkins to an audience unfamiliar with her career.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323033,"updateDate":1730147323033}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323033, 1730147323033),
    ($SATQ$5a5e22b5$SATQ$, $SATQ$854942d7-6f19-4ae6-a164-07d2094724e9$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$d83e830a-3660-46a3-9c22-79abde35de33$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Gravitational waves are powerful ripples that originate in deep space and eventually pass through Earth.</li>
<li>The Laser Interferometer Gravitational Wave Observatory (LIGO) is a physics study that began in 2002.</li>
<li>LIGO&rsquo;s goal is to detect and analyze gravitational waves.</li>
<li>LIGO uses a pair of massive gravitational wave detectors called interferometers that are thousands of miles apart.</li>
<li>In 2015, for the first time in history, LIGO researchers detected a gravitational wave passing through Earth.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to present LIGO&rsquo;s aim and methodology. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9aca26e0-33cb-41cf-8a0a-da511c697ce2","content":"<p>In 2015, LIGO&rsquo;s massive interferometers detected a powerful ripple that originated in deep space and eventually passed through Earth.</p>"},{"id":"58d763d5-14f4-445b-a5e5-f78004a4bf78","content":"<p>Though the physics study LIGO began in 2002, its massive interferometers didn&rsquo;t detect a gravitational wave until 2015.</p>"},{"id":"25c80a4b-221d-4dd4-9349-1ebb411302b0","content":"<p>To achieve its aims, LIGO uses a pair of massive interferometers that are thousands of miles apart.</p>"},{"id":"f34cfaae-2555-4571-a31d-edd8225bdb86","content":"<p>A physics study designed to detect and analyze gravitational waves, LIGO uses a pair of massive interferometers that are thousands of miles apart.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f34cfaae-2555-4571-a31d-edd8225bdb86"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence effectively presents the LIGO study&rsquo;s aim, noting that it is designed to detect and analyze gravitational waves, and its methodology (it uses two interferometers to detect the waves). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes a finding from the LIGO study; it doesn&rsquo;t effectively present the study&rsquo;s aim or its methodology. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence provides background information about the LIGO study&rsquo;s timeline; it doesn&rsquo;t effectively present the study&rsquo;s aim or its methodology. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence touches on LIGO&rsquo;s methodology, noting that it uses two interferometers, but doesn&rsquo;t indicate what the study&rsquo;s aims are.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-029-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$fb020452-09eb-4a81-b3d0-fd3b133f31af$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5a5e22b5","external_id":"854942d7-6f19-4ae6-a164-07d2094724e9","vaultid":"d83e830a-3660-46a3-9c22-79abde35de33","uId":"e199f54a-ac13-41fa-8a3a-c17f1ac4e2e7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gravitational waves are powerful ripples that originate in deep space and eventually pass through Earth.</li>\n<li>The Laser Interferometer Gravitational Wave Observatory (LIGO) is a physics study that began in 2002.</li>\n<li>LIGO&rsquo;s goal is to detect and analyze gravitational waves.</li>\n<li>LIGO uses a pair of massive gravitational wave detectors called interferometers that are thousands of miles apart.</li>\n<li>In 2015, for the first time in history, LIGO researchers detected a gravitational wave passing through Earth.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to present LIGO&rsquo;s aim and methodology. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9aca26e0-33cb-41cf-8a0a-da511c697ce2","content":"<p>In 2015, LIGO&rsquo;s massive interferometers detected a powerful ripple that originated in deep space and eventually passed through Earth.</p>"},{"id":"58d763d5-14f4-445b-a5e5-f78004a4bf78","content":"<p>Though the physics study LIGO began in 2002, its massive interferometers didn&rsquo;t detect a gravitational wave until 2015.</p>"},{"id":"25c80a4b-221d-4dd4-9349-1ebb411302b0","content":"<p>To achieve its aims, LIGO uses a pair of massive interferometers that are thousands of miles apart.</p>"},{"id":"f34cfaae-2555-4571-a31d-edd8225bdb86","content":"<p>A physics study designed to detect and analyze gravitational waves, LIGO uses a pair of massive interferometers that are thousands of miles apart.</p>"}],"keys":["f34cfaae-2555-4571-a31d-edd8225bdb86"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence effectively presents the LIGO study&rsquo;s aim, noting that it is designed to detect and analyze gravitational waves, and its methodology (it uses two interferometers to detect the waves). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes a finding from the LIGO study; it doesn&rsquo;t effectively present the study&rsquo;s aim or its methodology. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence provides background information about the LIGO study&rsquo;s timeline; it doesn&rsquo;t effectively present the study&rsquo;s aim or its methodology. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence touches on LIGO&rsquo;s methodology, noting that it uses two interferometers, but doesn&rsquo;t indicate what the study&rsquo;s aims are.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-029-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"fb020452-09eb-4a81-b3d0-fd3b133f31af","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959318,"updateDate":1691007959318}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959318, 1691007959318),
    ($SATQ$5b8b69a2$SATQ$, $SATQ$b587af2d-a137-4b31-a984-05d6e631b3ed$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$19f2ece0-d546-4798-aee0-c8c6e95bde37$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Archaeologist Jon Erlandson and colleagues argue that humans first arrived in the Americas by sea.</li>
<li>They propose that humans traveled between Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines from northeast Asia to the Americas.</li>
<li>Many of these islands and coastal zones were later submerged as glaciers melted and sea levels rose.</li>
<li>The researchers think that &ldquo;a coastal route, including kelp forests and estuaries, would have provided a rich mix of marine, estuarine, riverine, and terrestrial resources&rdquo; such as seaweeds, fish, and birds.</li>
<li>This proposed scenario is known as the kelp highway hypothesis.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to summarize the kelp highway hypothesis. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6ae93524-3021-49a0-8d33-5381c2479ebc","content":"<p>Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines likely contained &ldquo;a rich mix of marine, estuarine, riverine, and terrestrial resources&rdquo; such as seaweeds, fish, and birds, according to researchers.</p>"},{"id":"8c423133-8246-4e32-b24d-0b167fb4d0a7","content":"<p>One argument about how humans first arrived in the Americas is the kelp highway hypothesis proposed by Jon Erlandson and colleagues.</p>"},{"id":"707a9318-bc10-4d2f-954e-ed86efff3d06","content":"<p>Humans may have first arrived in the Americas by sea, traveling between Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines and subsisting on a variety of resources.</p>"},{"id":"3de9fa32-a055-481b-bafe-8984f44a8f66","content":"<p>As glaciers melted and sea levels rose, many Pacific Ocean islands and coastal zones were submerged.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["707a9318-bc10-4d2f-954e-ed86efff3d06"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. This choice summarizes the main idea of the kelp highway hypothesis, providing a high-level overview of how the hypothesis explains human migration to the Americas. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice describes one aspect of the proposed scenario but doesn&rsquo;t discuss human migration, which is the main focus of the kelp highway hypothesis. Choice B is incorrect. This choice introduces the kelp highway hypothesis but doesn&rsquo;t explain what it entails. Choice D is incorrect. This choice describes one element of the proposed scenario but doesn&rsquo;t discuss human migration, which is the main focus of the kelp highway hypothesis. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-SYN-H008$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$81738406-2224-467d-853f-a05ad1c475ac$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5b8b69a2","external_id":"b587af2d-a137-4b31-a984-05d6e631b3ed","vaultid":"19f2ece0-d546-4798-aee0-c8c6e95bde37","uId":"80b432e1-a5e4-4de0-a1ab-5ae314f216af","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Archaeologist Jon Erlandson and colleagues argue that humans first arrived in the Americas by sea.</li>\n<li>They propose that humans traveled between Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines from northeast Asia to the Americas.</li>\n<li>Many of these islands and coastal zones were later submerged as glaciers melted and sea levels rose.</li>\n<li>The researchers think that &ldquo;a coastal route, including kelp forests and estuaries, would have provided a rich mix of marine, estuarine, riverine, and terrestrial resources&rdquo; such as seaweeds, fish, and birds.</li>\n<li>This proposed scenario is known as the kelp highway hypothesis.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to summarize the kelp highway hypothesis. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6ae93524-3021-49a0-8d33-5381c2479ebc","content":"<p>Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines likely contained &ldquo;a rich mix of marine, estuarine, riverine, and terrestrial resources&rdquo; such as seaweeds, fish, and birds, according to researchers.</p>"},{"id":"8c423133-8246-4e32-b24d-0b167fb4d0a7","content":"<p>One argument about how humans first arrived in the Americas is the kelp highway hypothesis proposed by Jon Erlandson and colleagues.</p>"},{"id":"707a9318-bc10-4d2f-954e-ed86efff3d06","content":"<p>Humans may have first arrived in the Americas by sea, traveling between Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines and subsisting on a variety of resources.</p>"},{"id":"3de9fa32-a055-481b-bafe-8984f44a8f66","content":"<p>As glaciers melted and sea levels rose, many Pacific Ocean islands and coastal zones were submerged.</p>"}],"keys":["707a9318-bc10-4d2f-954e-ed86efff3d06"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. This choice summarizes the main idea of the kelp highway hypothesis, providing a high-level overview of how the hypothesis explains human migration to the Americas. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice describes one aspect of the proposed scenario but doesn&rsquo;t discuss human migration, which is the main focus of the kelp highway hypothesis. Choice B is incorrect. This choice introduces the kelp highway hypothesis but doesn&rsquo;t explain what it entails. Choice D is incorrect. This choice describes one element of the proposed scenario but doesn&rsquo;t discuss human migration, which is the main focus of the kelp highway hypothesis. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-SYN-H008","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"81738406-2224-467d-853f-a05ad1c475ac","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959372,"updateDate":1691007959372}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959372, 1691007959372),
    ($SATQ$5bb7dc03$SATQ$, $SATQ$d9b9f8f7-2ffc-420c-ad0e-b9bdbdc06d1a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f460b5a9-cbf1-453b-a9a2-4c42936d80ce$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Started in 1925, the Scripps National Spelling Bee is a US-based spelling competition.</li>
<li>The words used in the competition have diverse linguistic origins.</li>
<li>In 2008, Sameer Mishra won by correctly spelling the word &ldquo;guerdon.&rdquo;</li>
<li>&ldquo;Guerdon&rdquo; derives from the Anglo-French word &ldquo;guerdun.&rdquo;</li>
<li>In 2009, Kavya Shivashankar won by correctly spelling the word &ldquo;Laodicean.&rdquo;</li>
<li>&ldquo;Laodicean&rdquo; derives from the ancient Greek word &ldquo;<span lang="grc">Laod&iacute;keia.</span>&rdquo;</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize a difference in the origins of the two words. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c9ee08a2-1f99-4968-8f8f-9160089ec3de","content":"<p>&ldquo;Guerdon,&rdquo; the final word of the 2008 Scripps National Spelling Bee, is of Anglo-French origin, while the following year&rsquo;s final word, &ldquo;Laodicean,&rdquo; derives from ancient Greek.</p>"},{"id":"bfca8738-84ba-4afe-a314-4633e3c32a31","content":"<p>In 2008, Sameer Mishra won the Scripps National Spelling Bee by correctly spelling the word &ldquo;guerdon&rdquo;; however, the following year, Kavya Shivashankar won based on spelling the word &ldquo;Laodicean.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"4d214b13-348d-4944-a767-f761ec036038","content":"<p>Kavya Shivashankar won the 2009 Scripps National Spelling Bee by correctly spelling &ldquo;Laodicean,&rdquo; which derives from the ancient Greek word &ldquo;<span lang=\"grc\">Laod&iacute;keia.</span>&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"57e1bafd-4086-4bb9-97ff-3d3a1fd303cf","content":"<p>The Scripps National Spelling Bee uses words from diverse linguistic origins, such as &ldquo;guerdon&rdquo; and &ldquo;Laodicean.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c9ee08a2-1f99-4968-8f8f-9160089ec3de"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. Noting that &ldquo;guerdon&rdquo; is of Anglo-French origin and &ldquo;Laodicean&rdquo; is of ancient Greek origin, the sentence uses &ldquo;while&rdquo; to emphasize a difference in the origins of the two words. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence emphasizes two words used in the Scripps National Spelling Bee, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize (or mention) the words&rsquo; linguistic origins. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence specifies the linguistic origin of one word used in the Scripps National Spelling Bee, it doesn&rsquo;t mention the other word or emphasize a difference in the two words&rsquo; origins. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence makes a generalization about words used in the Scripps National Spelling Bee, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a difference in the words&rsquo; origins. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-708-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$0497a7c4-5960-47b6-a72a-63f32c27b031$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5bb7dc03","external_id":"d9b9f8f7-2ffc-420c-ad0e-b9bdbdc06d1a","vaultid":"f460b5a9-cbf1-453b-a9a2-4c42936d80ce","uId":"e4feead3-1645-4a51-8a22-34362f9a12ab","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Started in 1925, the Scripps National Spelling Bee is a US-based spelling competition.</li>\n<li>The words used in the competition have diverse linguistic origins.</li>\n<li>In 2008, Sameer Mishra won by correctly spelling the word &ldquo;guerdon.&rdquo;</li>\n<li>&ldquo;Guerdon&rdquo; derives from the Anglo-French word &ldquo;guerdun.&rdquo;</li>\n<li>In 2009, Kavya Shivashankar won by correctly spelling the word &ldquo;Laodicean.&rdquo;</li>\n<li>&ldquo;Laodicean&rdquo; derives from the ancient Greek word &ldquo;<span lang=\"grc\">Laod&iacute;keia.</span>&rdquo;</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize a difference in the origins of the two words. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c9ee08a2-1f99-4968-8f8f-9160089ec3de","content":"<p>&ldquo;Guerdon,&rdquo; the final word of the 2008 Scripps National Spelling Bee, is of Anglo-French origin, while the following year&rsquo;s final word, &ldquo;Laodicean,&rdquo; derives from ancient Greek.</p>"},{"id":"bfca8738-84ba-4afe-a314-4633e3c32a31","content":"<p>In 2008, Sameer Mishra won the Scripps National Spelling Bee by correctly spelling the word &ldquo;guerdon&rdquo;; however, the following year, Kavya Shivashankar won based on spelling the word &ldquo;Laodicean.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"4d214b13-348d-4944-a767-f761ec036038","content":"<p>Kavya Shivashankar won the 2009 Scripps National Spelling Bee by correctly spelling &ldquo;Laodicean,&rdquo; which derives from the ancient Greek word &ldquo;<span lang=\"grc\">Laod&iacute;keia.</span>&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"57e1bafd-4086-4bb9-97ff-3d3a1fd303cf","content":"<p>The Scripps National Spelling Bee uses words from diverse linguistic origins, such as &ldquo;guerdon&rdquo; and &ldquo;Laodicean.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["c9ee08a2-1f99-4968-8f8f-9160089ec3de"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. Noting that &ldquo;guerdon&rdquo; is of Anglo-French origin and &ldquo;Laodicean&rdquo; is of ancient Greek origin, the sentence uses &ldquo;while&rdquo; to emphasize a difference in the origins of the two words. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence emphasizes two words used in the Scripps National Spelling Bee, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize (or mention) the words&rsquo; linguistic origins. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence specifies the linguistic origin of one word used in the Scripps National Spelling Bee, it doesn&rsquo;t mention the other word or emphasize a difference in the two words&rsquo; origins. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence makes a generalization about words used in the Scripps National Spelling Bee, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a difference in the words&rsquo; origins. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-708-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"0497a7c4-5960-47b6-a72a-63f32c27b031","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959347,"updateDate":1691007959347}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959347, 1691007959347),
    ($SATQ$5c58cec0$SATQ$, $SATQ$02e0c152-44d7-473c-801f-33c3b32c565c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>To guarantee the validity of experimental results, scientists rely on precise, unchanging standards of measurement. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> metrologists (scientists who study measurement) developed the SI, or International System of Units. The SI&rsquo;s units of measurement are based on unchanging values in nature, such as the mass of an electron or the speed of light.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4e2db86f-d537-4bb2-a68b-3a345ed16a91","content":"<p>In contrast,</p>"},{"id":"3a1647bb-853a-4391-a5b2-93c0b430b896","content":"<p>Regardless,</p>"},{"id":"f02dc900-4263-4d8c-8d82-6c9ad3d4dbcf","content":"<p>In addition,</p>"},{"id":"455bd43b-1700-4867-8381-3c7a90d08e40","content":"<p>For this reason,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["455bd43b-1700-4867-8381-3c7a90d08e40"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. "For this reason" logically signals that the information that follows&mdash;that metrologists developed the SI based on unchanging values in nature&mdash;is a result of the previous claim that scientists rely on precise, unchanging standards of measurement to guarantee the validity of experimental results.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "in contrast" illogically signals that the information that follows contrasts with the previous claim that scientists rely on precise, unchanging standards of measurement. Instead, the information that metrologists developed the SI based on unchanging values in nature is a result of that claim. Choice B is incorrect because "regardless" illogically signals that the information that follows is true despite the previous claim that scientists rely on precise, unchanging standards of measure. Instead, the information that metrologists developed the SI based on unchanging values in nature is a result of that claim. Choice C is incorrect because "in addition" illogically signals that the information that follows is merely an additional fact related to the previous claim that scientists rely on precise, unchanging standards of measurement. Instead, the information that metrologists developed the SI based on unchanging values in nature is a result of that claim.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5c58cec0","external_id":"02e0c152-44d7-473c-801f-33c3b32c565c","vaultid":null,"uId":"9ffe3d77-1a85-4f9d-a458-62933cdc4d0c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>To guarantee the validity of experimental results, scientists rely on precise, unchanging standards of measurement. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> metrologists (scientists who study measurement) developed the SI, or International System of Units. The SI&rsquo;s units of measurement are based on unchanging values in nature, such as the mass of an electron or the speed of light.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4e2db86f-d537-4bb2-a68b-3a345ed16a91","content":"<p>In contrast,</p>"},{"id":"3a1647bb-853a-4391-a5b2-93c0b430b896","content":"<p>Regardless,</p>"},{"id":"f02dc900-4263-4d8c-8d82-6c9ad3d4dbcf","content":"<p>In addition,</p>"},{"id":"455bd43b-1700-4867-8381-3c7a90d08e40","content":"<p>For this reason,</p>"}],"keys":["455bd43b-1700-4867-8381-3c7a90d08e40"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. \"For this reason\" logically signals that the information that follows&mdash;that metrologists developed the SI based on unchanging values in nature&mdash;is a result of the previous claim that scientists rely on precise, unchanging standards of measurement to guarantee the validity of experimental results.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"in contrast\" illogically signals that the information that follows contrasts with the previous claim that scientists rely on precise, unchanging standards of measurement. Instead, the information that metrologists developed the SI based on unchanging values in nature is a result of that claim. Choice B is incorrect because \"regardless\" illogically signals that the information that follows is true despite the previous claim that scientists rely on precise, unchanging standards of measure. Instead, the information that metrologists developed the SI based on unchanging values in nature is a result of that claim. Choice C is incorrect because \"in addition\" illogically signals that the information that follows is merely an additional fact related to the previous claim that scientists rely on precise, unchanging standards of measurement. Instead, the information that metrologists developed the SI based on unchanging values in nature is a result of that claim.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323042,"updateDate":1730147323042}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323042, 1730147323042),
    ($SATQ$5d3177aa$SATQ$, $SATQ$c8d42223-bd50-4837-a884-755ec29e7a39$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$1a284328-ba1d-4084-a066-63aa75a22808$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>In the early 1960s, the US had a strict national-origins quota system for immigrants.</li>
<li>The number of new immigrants allowed from a country each year was based on how many people from that country lived in the US in 1890.</li>
<li>This system favored immigrants from northern Europe.</li>
<li>Almost 70% of slots were reserved for immigrants from Great Britain, Ireland, and Germany.</li>
<li>The 1965 Hart-Celler Act abolished the national-origins quota system.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to present the significance of the Hart-Celler Act to an audience unfamiliar with the history of US immigration. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"814794ca-289a-4e23-95c2-fc6889055a9e","content":"<p>Almost 70% of slots were reserved for immigrants from Great Britain, Ireland, and Germany at the time the Hart-Celler Act was proposed.</p>"},{"id":"853a366f-75c4-4a91-8d1a-0ecc3b743c81","content":"<p>Prior to the Hart-Celler Act, new immigration quotas were based on how many people from each country lived in the US in 1890.</p>"},{"id":"1490fdd0-e159-4405-8ec9-c2dbc8d2eadc","content":"<p>The quota system in place in the early 1960s was abolished by the 1965 Hart-Celler Act.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"05efd54e-8bed-4072-b457-c90036669905","content":"<p>The 1965 Hart-Celler Act abolished the national-origins quota system, which favored immigrants from northern Europe.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["05efd54e-8bed-4072-b457-c90036669905"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence presents the significance of the Hart-Celler Act to an audience unfamiliar with the history of US immigration, noting that the 1965 act abolished the national-origins quota system and explaining why that mattered, historically: because the old quota system had favored immigrants from northern Europe. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes an aspect of immigration policy at the time the Hart-Celler Act was proposed; it doesn&rsquo;t present the significance of the Hart-Celler Act to an audience unfamiliar with the history of US immigration. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence describes an aspect of immigration policy before the Hart-Celler Act; it doesn&rsquo;t describe or present the significance of the act to an audience unfamiliar with the history of US immigration. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence indicates that the Hart-Celler Act abolished the old quota system, it doesn&rsquo;t explain the act or the quota system to an audience unfamiliar with the history of US immigration. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-787-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$7a5bcf69-1d64-4bad-a1fb-97ba262319bf$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5d3177aa","external_id":"c8d42223-bd50-4837-a884-755ec29e7a39","vaultid":"1a284328-ba1d-4084-a066-63aa75a22808","uId":"7163f7c0-4491-4882-83cc-4e76f593cfff","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the early 1960s, the US had a strict national-origins quota system for immigrants.</li>\n<li>The number of new immigrants allowed from a country each year was based on how many people from that country lived in the US in 1890.</li>\n<li>This system favored immigrants from northern Europe.</li>\n<li>Almost 70% of slots were reserved for immigrants from Great Britain, Ireland, and Germany.</li>\n<li>The 1965 Hart-Celler Act abolished the national-origins quota system.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to present the significance of the Hart-Celler Act to an audience unfamiliar with the history of US immigration. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"814794ca-289a-4e23-95c2-fc6889055a9e","content":"<p>Almost 70% of slots were reserved for immigrants from Great Britain, Ireland, and Germany at the time the Hart-Celler Act was proposed.</p>"},{"id":"853a366f-75c4-4a91-8d1a-0ecc3b743c81","content":"<p>Prior to the Hart-Celler Act, new immigration quotas were based on how many people from each country lived in the US in 1890.</p>"},{"id":"1490fdd0-e159-4405-8ec9-c2dbc8d2eadc","content":"<p>The quota system in place in the early 1960s was abolished by the 1965 Hart-Celler Act.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"05efd54e-8bed-4072-b457-c90036669905","content":"<p>The 1965 Hart-Celler Act abolished the national-origins quota system, which favored immigrants from northern Europe.</p>"}],"keys":["05efd54e-8bed-4072-b457-c90036669905"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence presents the significance of the Hart-Celler Act to an audience unfamiliar with the history of US immigration, noting that the 1965 act abolished the national-origins quota system and explaining why that mattered, historically: because the old quota system had favored immigrants from northern Europe. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes an aspect of immigration policy at the time the Hart-Celler Act was proposed; it doesn&rsquo;t present the significance of the Hart-Celler Act to an audience unfamiliar with the history of US immigration. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence describes an aspect of immigration policy before the Hart-Celler Act; it doesn&rsquo;t describe or present the significance of the act to an audience unfamiliar with the history of US immigration. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence indicates that the Hart-Celler Act abolished the old quota system, it doesn&rsquo;t explain the act or the quota system to an audience unfamiliar with the history of US immigration. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-787-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"7a5bcf69-1d64-4bad-a1fb-97ba262319bf","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959353,"updateDate":1691007959353}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959353, 1691007959353),
    ($SATQ$5e93039f$SATQ$, $SATQ$0d3516d6-3000-4f47-a632-b90fa2068df3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Roughly once an hour, a torrent of boiling water shoots up 100 feet or more from Yellowstone&rsquo;s Old Faithful geyser before plunging back to the surface&mdash;a cycle seemingly inhospitable to life. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> as microbiologist Eric Boyd attests, &ldquo;the geyser is&hellip;almost like a cradle for biodiversity,&rdquo; home to numerous bacteria species that thrive in its sulfurous waters.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"93113fe5-3562-43b6-94f4-8d5823300bd4","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"},{"id":"f86c28e4-c9c9-418e-abcd-3454194e3362","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"3dd119c6-0c79-4bfd-a91d-11738c28e8c7","content":"<p>Still,</p>"},{"id":"e6d4e710-9f36-4dfa-a49a-61296decb126","content":"<p>In other words,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3dd119c6-0c79-4bfd-a91d-11738c28e8c7"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. "Still" logically signals that the information in this sentence about Old Faithful&rsquo;s thriving bacteria species is true despite the previous claim that conditions at the geyser seem as if they would be inhospitable to life.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "thus" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a result or consequence of the previous claim about Old Faithful&rsquo;s seemingly inhospitable conditions. Instead, this information about the geyser&rsquo;s many thriving bacteria species is true despite the previous claim. Choice B is incorrect because "specifically" illogically signals that the information in this sentence provides specific, precise details elaborating on the previous claim about Old Faithful&rsquo;s seemingly inhospitable conditions. Instead, this information about the geyser&rsquo;s many thriving bacteria species is true despite the previous claim. Choice D is incorrect because "in other words" illogically signals that the information in this sentence serves as a paraphrase or restatement of the previous claim about Old Faithful&rsquo;s seemingly inhospitable conditions. Instead, this information about the geyser&rsquo;s many thriving bacteria species is true despite the previous claim.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5e93039f","external_id":"0d3516d6-3000-4f47-a632-b90fa2068df3","vaultid":null,"uId":"b0a2b15a-8885-494b-83aa-0207bb50ddf3","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Roughly once an hour, a torrent of boiling water shoots up 100 feet or more from Yellowstone&rsquo;s Old Faithful geyser before plunging back to the surface&mdash;a cycle seemingly inhospitable to life. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> as microbiologist Eric Boyd attests, &ldquo;the geyser is&hellip;almost like a cradle for biodiversity,&rdquo; home to numerous bacteria species that thrive in its sulfurous waters.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"93113fe5-3562-43b6-94f4-8d5823300bd4","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"},{"id":"f86c28e4-c9c9-418e-abcd-3454194e3362","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"3dd119c6-0c79-4bfd-a91d-11738c28e8c7","content":"<p>Still,</p>"},{"id":"e6d4e710-9f36-4dfa-a49a-61296decb126","content":"<p>In other words,</p>"}],"keys":["3dd119c6-0c79-4bfd-a91d-11738c28e8c7"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. \"Still\" logically signals that the information in this sentence about Old Faithful&rsquo;s thriving bacteria species is true despite the previous claim that conditions at the geyser seem as if they would be inhospitable to life.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"thus\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a result or consequence of the previous claim about Old Faithful&rsquo;s seemingly inhospitable conditions. Instead, this information about the geyser&rsquo;s many thriving bacteria species is true despite the previous claim. Choice B is incorrect because \"specifically\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence provides specific, precise details elaborating on the previous claim about Old Faithful&rsquo;s seemingly inhospitable conditions. Instead, this information about the geyser&rsquo;s many thriving bacteria species is true despite the previous claim. Choice D is incorrect because \"in other words\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence serves as a paraphrase or restatement of the previous claim about Old Faithful&rsquo;s seemingly inhospitable conditions. Instead, this information about the geyser&rsquo;s many thriving bacteria species is true despite the previous claim.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669203,"updateDate":1743430669203}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669203, 1743430669203),
    ($SATQ$5fa51c86$SATQ$, $SATQ$f0ebdaaf-0942-435d-9436-45fbbbf064ee$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$9f33a5bb-fcfc-4b74-8b7a-9ab38e124dde$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Ulaanbaatar is the capital of Mongolia.</li>
<li>The city&rsquo;s population is 907,802.</li>
<li>Ulaanbaatar contains 31.98 percent of Mongolia&rsquo;s population.</li>
<li>Hanoi is the capital of Vietnam.</li>
<li>The city&rsquo;s population is 7,781,631.</li>
<li>Hanoi contains 8.14 percent of Vietnam&rsquo;s population.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize the relative sizes of the two capitals&rsquo; populations. Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to emphasize the relative sizes of the two capitals&rsquo; populations?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3efc02ec-1aa5-4b8b-91df-430c49825bc7","content":"<p>Mongolia&rsquo;s capital is Ulaanbaatar, which has 907,802 people, and Vietnam&rsquo;s capital is Hanoi, which has 7,781,631 people.</p>"},{"id":"a5e4c698-9485-47aa-882a-ebc1806fa796","content":"<p>Comparing Vietnam and Mongolia, 7,781,631 is 8.14 percent of Vietnam&rsquo;s population, and 907,802 is 31.98 percent of Mongolia&rsquo;s.</p>"},{"id":"afde2e3b-c877-4954-b545-d04393b3cfd9","content":"<p>Even though Hanoi (population 7,781,631) is larger than Ulaanbaatar (population 907,802), Ulaanbaatar accounts for more of its country&rsquo;s population.</p>"},{"id":"e0c48c0a-a3d9-41cc-a29a-6addbb0d6296","content":"<p>The populations of the capitals of Mongolia and Vietnam are 907,802 (Ulaanbaatar) and 7,781,631 (Hanoi), respectively.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["afde2e3b-c877-4954-b545-d04393b3cfd9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the relative sizes of the capital cities&rsquo; populations, noting that even though Hanoi has more people overall, Ulaanbaatar accounts for a larger percentage of the people in its country. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence indicates the population size of each capital, it fails to emphasize their sizes relative to each other or to their countries&rsquo; overall population sizes. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the population sizes of the two countries; it fails to mention the capitals. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence indicates the population size of each capital, it fails to emphasize their sizes relative to each other or to their countries&rsquo; overall population sizes. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-713-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$fe47c1d6-342c-4a70-b2b9-b28d4184adb5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5fa51c86","external_id":"f0ebdaaf-0942-435d-9436-45fbbbf064ee","vaultid":"9f33a5bb-fcfc-4b74-8b7a-9ab38e124dde","uId":"702777fe-2de8-48ef-858b-1f67dab9fef9","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ulaanbaatar is the capital of Mongolia.</li>\n<li>The city&rsquo;s population is 907,802.</li>\n<li>Ulaanbaatar contains 31.98 percent of Mongolia&rsquo;s population.</li>\n<li>Hanoi is the capital of Vietnam.</li>\n<li>The city&rsquo;s population is 7,781,631.</li>\n<li>Hanoi contains 8.14 percent of Vietnam&rsquo;s population.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize the relative sizes of the two capitals&rsquo; populations. Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to emphasize the relative sizes of the two capitals&rsquo; populations?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3efc02ec-1aa5-4b8b-91df-430c49825bc7","content":"<p>Mongolia&rsquo;s capital is Ulaanbaatar, which has 907,802 people, and Vietnam&rsquo;s capital is Hanoi, which has 7,781,631 people.</p>"},{"id":"a5e4c698-9485-47aa-882a-ebc1806fa796","content":"<p>Comparing Vietnam and Mongolia, 7,781,631 is 8.14 percent of Vietnam&rsquo;s population, and 907,802 is 31.98 percent of Mongolia&rsquo;s.</p>"},{"id":"afde2e3b-c877-4954-b545-d04393b3cfd9","content":"<p>Even though Hanoi (population 7,781,631) is larger than Ulaanbaatar (population 907,802), Ulaanbaatar accounts for more of its country&rsquo;s population.</p>"},{"id":"e0c48c0a-a3d9-41cc-a29a-6addbb0d6296","content":"<p>The populations of the capitals of Mongolia and Vietnam are 907,802 (Ulaanbaatar) and 7,781,631 (Hanoi), respectively.</p>"}],"keys":["afde2e3b-c877-4954-b545-d04393b3cfd9"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the relative sizes of the capital cities&rsquo; populations, noting that even though Hanoi has more people overall, Ulaanbaatar accounts for a larger percentage of the people in its country. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence indicates the population size of each capital, it fails to emphasize their sizes relative to each other or to their countries&rsquo; overall population sizes. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the population sizes of the two countries; it fails to mention the capitals. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence indicates the population size of each capital, it fails to emphasize their sizes relative to each other or to their countries&rsquo; overall population sizes. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-713-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"fe47c1d6-342c-4a70-b2b9-b28d4184adb5","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959348,"updateDate":1691007959348}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959348, 1691007959348),
    ($SATQ$601b9d18$SATQ$, $SATQ$cc59c3a6-c63d-42cd-bc64-889005894301$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$24932f8f-e771-4d0f-b4dd-8075b3cee877$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Some members of the US Supreme Court have resisted calls to televise the court&rsquo;s oral arguments, concerned that the participants would be tempted to perform for the cameras (and thus lower the quality of the discourse). <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the justices worry that most viewers would not even watch the full deliberations, only short clips that could be misinterpreted and mischaracterized.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7d1d6bc4-9c6a-4f64-a34b-771ca32f98cb","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"7cf33e36-b866-4f2a-adef-e35a564b6b84","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"828c46e4-6397-487d-8568-9766bf337adf","content":"<p>In comparison,</p>"},{"id":"a06756e8-8938-47f3-81a9-4b3a6cdadc6a","content":"<p>For example,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7cf33e36-b866-4f2a-adef-e35a564b6b84"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Additionally&rdquo; logically signals that the claim in this sentence&mdash;that some Supreme Court justices worry that viewers (of televised court arguments) would watch only short, misleading clips&mdash;adds to the information in the previous sentence. Specifically, the previous sentence indicates one concern raised by those opposed to televising the court&rsquo;s oral arguments, and the claim that follows indicates a second, additional concern. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;however&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence contrasts with the information in the previous sentence. Instead, the claim adds to the information, indicating a second, additional concern that some Supreme Court justices have about televising the court&rsquo;s arguments. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;in comparison&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is being compared to the information in the previous sentence. Instead, the claim adds to the information, indicating a second, additional concern that some Supreme Court justices have about televising the court&rsquo;s arguments. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence exemplifies the information in the previous sentence. Instead, the claim adds to the information, indicating a second, additional concern that some Supreme Court justices have about televising the court&rsquo;s arguments. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-213-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$3caa7127-181b-4e11-a26a-635e937f1b5a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"601b9d18","external_id":"cc59c3a6-c63d-42cd-bc64-889005894301","vaultid":"24932f8f-e771-4d0f-b4dd-8075b3cee877","uId":"0eeaacfb-456b-4568-94e4-50b3193e7807","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Some members of the US Supreme Court have resisted calls to televise the court&rsquo;s oral arguments, concerned that the participants would be tempted to perform for the cameras (and thus lower the quality of the discourse). <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the justices worry that most viewers would not even watch the full deliberations, only short clips that could be misinterpreted and mischaracterized.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7d1d6bc4-9c6a-4f64-a34b-771ca32f98cb","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"7cf33e36-b866-4f2a-adef-e35a564b6b84","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"828c46e4-6397-487d-8568-9766bf337adf","content":"<p>In comparison,</p>"},{"id":"a06756e8-8938-47f3-81a9-4b3a6cdadc6a","content":"<p>For example,</p>"}],"keys":["7cf33e36-b866-4f2a-adef-e35a564b6b84"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Additionally&rdquo; logically signals that the claim in this sentence&mdash;that some Supreme Court justices worry that viewers (of televised court arguments) would watch only short, misleading clips&mdash;adds to the information in the previous sentence. Specifically, the previous sentence indicates one concern raised by those opposed to televising the court&rsquo;s oral arguments, and the claim that follows indicates a second, additional concern. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;however&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence contrasts with the information in the previous sentence. Instead, the claim adds to the information, indicating a second, additional concern that some Supreme Court justices have about televising the court&rsquo;s arguments. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;in comparison&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is being compared to the information in the previous sentence. Instead, the claim adds to the information, indicating a second, additional concern that some Supreme Court justices have about televising the court&rsquo;s arguments. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence exemplifies the information in the previous sentence. Instead, the claim adds to the information, indicating a second, additional concern that some Supreme Court justices have about televising the court&rsquo;s arguments. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-213-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"3caa7127-181b-4e11-a26a-635e937f1b5a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959327,"updateDate":1691007959327}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959327, 1691007959327),
    ($SATQ$6036ad0e$SATQ$, $SATQ$2c43b532-5d8b-4df1-9313-337c17276300$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 2014, Nestor Gomez won his first-ever storytelling competition, relating a tale about his life as a Guatemalan immigrant living in Chicago. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in 2017, Gomez created the show <em>80 Minutes Around the World</em> as a platform for others to share stories about their immigration experiences.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"18d37849-6e40-49d6-9728-5cd1e2ef59c5","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"},{"id":"587ae280-bccc-4ebd-807e-10536a4e059d","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"47db25d1-8daa-4453-bbd8-ad8ca4c648b8","content":"<p>Later,</p>"},{"id":"a7c6a071-278c-4556-bba6-cde394237c28","content":"<p>In other words,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["47db25d1-8daa-4453-bbd8-ad8ca4c648b8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. "Later" logically signals that the information in the sentence&mdash;that in 2017 Gomez created a platform for others to share stories about their immigration experiences&mdash;occurs later in a chronological series of events than the previous information about Gomez winning his first storytelling competition in 2014.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "instead" illogically signals that Gomez created a platform for others to share stories about their immigration experiences as an alternative to winning his first storytelling competition. Rather, Gomez created the platform later&mdash;in a chronological series of events&mdash;than when he won the competition. Choice B is incorrect because "for example" illogically signals that the information about Gomez creating a platform for others to share stories exemplifies his winning his first storytelling competition. Rather, Gomez created the platform later&mdash;in a chronological series of events&mdash;than when he won the competition. Choice D is incorrect because "in other words" illogically signals that the information about Gomez creating a platform for others to share stories is merely a paraphrase or restatement of the previous information about Gomez winning his first storytelling competition. Rather, Gomez created the platform later&mdash;in a chronological series of events&mdash;than when he won the competition.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6036ad0e","external_id":"2c43b532-5d8b-4df1-9313-337c17276300","vaultid":null,"uId":"b7216638-3625-4bc2-9adc-a6d475578c35","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 2014, Nestor Gomez won his first-ever storytelling competition, relating a tale about his life as a Guatemalan immigrant living in Chicago. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in 2017, Gomez created the show <em>80 Minutes Around the World</em> as a platform for others to share stories about their immigration experiences.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"18d37849-6e40-49d6-9728-5cd1e2ef59c5","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"},{"id":"587ae280-bccc-4ebd-807e-10536a4e059d","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"47db25d1-8daa-4453-bbd8-ad8ca4c648b8","content":"<p>Later,</p>"},{"id":"a7c6a071-278c-4556-bba6-cde394237c28","content":"<p>In other words,</p>"}],"keys":["47db25d1-8daa-4453-bbd8-ad8ca4c648b8"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. \"Later\" logically signals that the information in the sentence&mdash;that in 2017 Gomez created a platform for others to share stories about their immigration experiences&mdash;occurs later in a chronological series of events than the previous information about Gomez winning his first storytelling competition in 2014.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"instead\" illogically signals that Gomez created a platform for others to share stories about their immigration experiences as an alternative to winning his first storytelling competition. Rather, Gomez created the platform later&mdash;in a chronological series of events&mdash;than when he won the competition. Choice B is incorrect because \"for example\" illogically signals that the information about Gomez creating a platform for others to share stories exemplifies his winning his first storytelling competition. Rather, Gomez created the platform later&mdash;in a chronological series of events&mdash;than when he won the competition. Choice D is incorrect because \"in other words\" illogically signals that the information about Gomez creating a platform for others to share stories is merely a paraphrase or restatement of the previous information about Gomez winning his first storytelling competition. Rather, Gomez created the platform later&mdash;in a chronological series of events&mdash;than when he won the competition.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323043,"updateDate":1730147323043}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323043, 1730147323043),
    ($SATQ$6081831f$SATQ$, $SATQ$95c4ec98-4d3b-4f64-89bd-c7458d4215e4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f5616238-ff3b-4ffe-bb42-57a538dfe257$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>When Chinese director Chlo&eacute; Zhao accepted the Oscar in 2021 for her film <em>Nomadland</em>, she made Academy Award history. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> only one other woman, Kathryn Bigelow of the United States, had been named best director at the Oscars, making Zhao the second woman and the first Asian woman to win the award.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4bc215d7-5ab8-4a71-9f81-fe053756ba81","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"dfaaa2e8-c043-4e8c-ab27-9a1091859961","content":"<p>Previously,</p>"},{"id":"dd840f01-355a-4cc2-9234-8f5791668252","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"d07b52b8-1c6b-43e9-a7a9-9ef8086ddda3","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["dfaaa2e8-c043-4e8c-ab27-9a1091859961"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Previously&rdquo; logically signals that the event described in this sentence&mdash;Bigelow being named best director&mdash;occurred before Zhao&rsquo;s win. The fact that only one other woman had won the award before puts Zhao&rsquo;s win in perspective. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;as a result&rdquo; illogically signals that the event described in this sentence occurred as a result or consequence of Zhao&rsquo;s win. Instead, it occurred before Zhao was named best director and puts Zhao&rsquo;s win in perspective. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;however&rdquo; illogically signals that the event described in this sentence occurred in spite of or in contrast to Zhao&rsquo;s win. Instead, it occurred before Zhao was named best director and puts Zhao&rsquo;s win in perspective. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;likewise&rdquo; illogically signals that this sentence merely adds a second, similar piece of information to the information about Zhao&rsquo;s win. Instead, the fact that only one other woman had won the award before puts Zhao&rsquo;s win in perspective. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-210-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$1bc9e637-f7a2-4932-9a4e-01ab43eafd9d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"6081831f","external_id":"95c4ec98-4d3b-4f64-89bd-c7458d4215e4","vaultid":"f5616238-ff3b-4ffe-bb42-57a538dfe257","uId":"4ecbc121-9116-4c29-89d9-83e4c8e1f085","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>When Chinese director Chlo&eacute; Zhao accepted the Oscar in 2021 for her film <em>Nomadland</em>, she made Academy Award history. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> only one other woman, Kathryn Bigelow of the United States, had been named best director at the Oscars, making Zhao the second woman and the first Asian woman to win the award.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4bc215d7-5ab8-4a71-9f81-fe053756ba81","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"dfaaa2e8-c043-4e8c-ab27-9a1091859961","content":"<p>Previously,</p>"},{"id":"dd840f01-355a-4cc2-9234-8f5791668252","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"d07b52b8-1c6b-43e9-a7a9-9ef8086ddda3","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"}],"keys":["dfaaa2e8-c043-4e8c-ab27-9a1091859961"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Previously&rdquo; logically signals that the event described in this sentence&mdash;Bigelow being named best director&mdash;occurred before Zhao&rsquo;s win. The fact that only one other woman had won the award before puts Zhao&rsquo;s win in perspective. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;as a result&rdquo; illogically signals that the event described in this sentence occurred as a result or consequence of Zhao&rsquo;s win. Instead, it occurred before Zhao was named best director and puts Zhao&rsquo;s win in perspective. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;however&rdquo; illogically signals that the event described in this sentence occurred in spite of or in contrast to Zhao&rsquo;s win. Instead, it occurred before Zhao was named best director and puts Zhao&rsquo;s win in perspective. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;likewise&rdquo; illogically signals that this sentence merely adds a second, similar piece of information to the information about Zhao&rsquo;s win. Instead, the fact that only one other woman had won the award before puts Zhao&rsquo;s win in perspective. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-210-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"1bc9e637-f7a2-4932-9a4e-01ab43eafd9d","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959326,"updateDate":1691007959326}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959326, 1691007959326),
    ($SATQ$60917233$SATQ$, $SATQ$c8726d4a-0baa-4c7c-a1b5-9351e4a4832d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In the 1880s, inventor Lewis Latimer improved upon Thomas Edison&rsquo;s design for the electric light bulb. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Latimer made the light bulb more durable by placing cardboard around its carbon filament. With this innovation, Latimer became the first Black inventor to contribute to the electrification of the world.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f37d7c96-3688-42a7-8738-98cfd36098db","content":"<p>Soon,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"a01e937f-ebdf-4946-bb6e-001fb503dfc6","content":"<p>Regardless,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"e3749992-5ddb-4ef9-bd1a-28e2fc2605c0","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"5aa790ab-99b4-4893-8bd6-d703019a7884","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5aa790ab-99b4-4893-8bd6-d703019a7884"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. "Specifically" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;Latimer making the light bulb more durable&mdash;provides a specific detail elaborating on the more general claim in the previous sentence that Latimer improved the light bulb.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "soon" illogically signals that the information in this sentence occurred shortly after Latimer improved the light bulb. Instead, Latimer making the bulb more durable was the specific improvement. Choice B is incorrect because "regardless" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is true despite the previous claim about Latimer. Instead, the information about Latimer making the bulb more durable provides a specific detail elaborating on that claim. Choice C is incorrect because "however" illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the previous claim about Latimer. Instead, the information about Latimer making the bulb more durable provides a specific detail elaborating on that claim.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"60917233","external_id":"c8726d4a-0baa-4c7c-a1b5-9351e4a4832d","vaultid":null,"uId":"0fa08ad7-874f-488d-a5ff-8ad52fcfb0ef","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In the 1880s, inventor Lewis Latimer improved upon Thomas Edison&rsquo;s design for the electric light bulb. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Latimer made the light bulb more durable by placing cardboard around its carbon filament. With this innovation, Latimer became the first Black inventor to contribute to the electrification of the world.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f37d7c96-3688-42a7-8738-98cfd36098db","content":"<p>Soon,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"a01e937f-ebdf-4946-bb6e-001fb503dfc6","content":"<p>Regardless,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"e3749992-5ddb-4ef9-bd1a-28e2fc2605c0","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"5aa790ab-99b4-4893-8bd6-d703019a7884","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"}],"keys":["5aa790ab-99b4-4893-8bd6-d703019a7884"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. \"Specifically\" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;Latimer making the light bulb more durable&mdash;provides a specific detail elaborating on the more general claim in the previous sentence that Latimer improved the light bulb.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"soon\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence occurred shortly after Latimer improved the light bulb. Instead, Latimer making the bulb more durable was the specific improvement. Choice B is incorrect because \"regardless\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is true despite the previous claim about Latimer. Instead, the information about Latimer making the bulb more durable provides a specific detail elaborating on that claim. Choice C is incorrect because \"however\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the previous claim about Latimer. Instead, the information about Latimer making the bulb more durable provides a specific detail elaborating on that claim.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323043,"updateDate":1730147323043}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323043, 1730147323043),
    ($SATQ$61c0f7b3$SATQ$, $SATQ$2cebbebd-329d-4b4b-9843-e00858aed019$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<ul>
<li>In 2017, a research team led by Mary Caswell Stoddard determined the average lengths of eggs produced by various bird species.</li>
<li><em>Gygis alba</em> is a species of bird in the order Charadriiformes.</li>
<li><em>Gygis alba </em>eggs had an average length of 4.46 cm.</li>
<li><em>Gavia stellata </em>is a species of bird in the order Gaviiformes.</li>
<li><em>Gavia stellata </em>eggs had an average length of 7.22 cm.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to emphasize a difference between the eggs of the two species?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"dab27961-379d-4d5e-a654-7dd229e0bb1f","content":"<p>A 2017 study compared the lengths of eggs produced by an array of different bird species, such as <em>Gygis alba</em> and <em>Gavia stellata</em>.</p>"},{"id":"6d9fff1d-8674-42fd-b97c-d6c0776d95d6","content":"<p>A 2017 study found that <em>Gygis alba</em> eggs had an average length of 4.46 cm, whereas <em>Gavia stellata</em> eggs were longer, with an average length of 7.22 cm.</p>"},{"id":"9603f621-c227-44dd-9443-6c894f25b5d9","content":"<p>The bird species <em>Gygis alba</em>, which belongs to the order Charadriiformes, and <em>Gavia stellata</em>, of the order Gaviiformes, were included in a 2017 study that compared the average lengths of their eggs.</p>"},{"id":"ad111ce1-1022-41f1-befa-195ecff09b17","content":"<p>Mary Caswell Stoddard led a research study that determined the average lengths of eggs, including those of <em>Gygis alba</em> birds (4.46 cm) and <em>Gavia stellata</em> birds (7.22 cm).</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6d9fff1d-8674-42fd-b97c-d6c0776d95d6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes a difference between the eggs of the two species, directly contrasting the shorter average length of <em>Gygis alba</em> eggs (4.46 cm) with the longer average length of <em>Gavia stellata</em> eggs (7.22 cm).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence mentions both bird species, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a difference between their eggs. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about the two bird species, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a difference between their eggs. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence provides the average lengths of both species&rsquo; eggs, it doesn&rsquo;t explicitly emphasize the difference between them.</p>$SATQ$, false, 